\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Why Unions Now Struggle To Expand\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Eavis \par \uc2 1,529 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  has made little headway in organizing workers at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  despite wage and other gains it secured at \uc2 UPS\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 Last year, two unions representing workers at three large automakers and \uc2 UPS\uc2  negotiated new labor contracts that included big raises and other gains. Leaders of the unions -- the \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  and the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  -- hoped the wins would help them organize workers across their industry.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. won one vote to unionize a \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  factory in Tennessee last month and lost one this month at two \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  plants in Alabama. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have made even less progress at \uc2 UPS\uc2 's big nonunion rivals in the delivery business, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 FedEx\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Polling shows that public support for unions is the highest it has been in decades. But labor experts said structural forces would make it hard for labor groups to increase their membership, which is the lowest it has been as a percentage of the total work force in decades. Unions also face stiff opposition from many employers and conservative political leaders.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  provide an instructive case study. Many of the workers doing deliveries for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  work for contractors, typically small and medium-size businesses that can be hard to organize. And delivery workers employed directly by \uc2 FedEx\uc2  in its Express business are governed by a labor law that requires unions to organize all similar workers at the company nationally at once -- a tougher standard than the one that applies to organizing employees at automakers, \uc2 UPS\uc2  and other employers.\par \par \uc2 Some labor experts also said the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  had not made as forceful a push as the U.A.W. to organize nonunion workers after securing a new contract with \uc2 UPS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''You didn't have that energy that you saw with the U.A.W.'s leaders,'' said Jake Rosenfeld, a sociologist who studies labor at \uc2 Washington University in St. Louis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  officials said the \uc2 UPS\uc2  deal, which increases the average annual compensation, including benefits, of a \uc2 UPS\uc2  driver to $170,000 from $145,000, was helping them gain members. At \uc2 DHL\uc2 , a delivery company where the union has long had a big presence, the union added 1,100 members last year and is pushing to gain another 1,500. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  are also pursuing a legal challenge against \uc2 Amazon\uc2  that could allow them to gain ground at the company and its contractors.\par \par \uc2 ''It's been very helpful for us to mobilize,'' \uc2 Sean O'Brien\uc2 , the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' president, said in an interview, referring to the \uc2 UPS\uc2  contract. ''We've set the standard in the industry.''\par \par \uc2 But the union has also suffered losses. Yellow, a trucking company that employed 24,000 \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , shut down and filed for bankruptcy protection last year.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  said they were confident in their approach to managing and compensating workers. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said it had made investments that bolstered pay and benefits at its delivery contractors. \uc2 FedEx\uc2  said its nonunion model allowed it to quickly increase pay whereas \uc2 UPS\uc2 's union employees were bound by the terms of five-year contracts.\par \par \uc2 ''Our culture, built and tested over 50 years, is based on the philosophy that if we take care of our people, they will deliver outstanding service for our customers, which will drive business results for our company,'' Tracy Brightman, \uc2 FedEx\uc2 's chief people officer, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Around 310,000 \uc2 UPS\uc2  employees belong to the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . Many of them see \uc2 FedEx\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  drivers on their routes and talk about pay, benefits and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 ''We make much more money than anyone else in the industry,'' said Essence Carlisle, a part-time package handler at \uc2 UPS\uc2 's hub in Louisville, Ky. ''I definitely intend to make a career here.''\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UPS\uc2  deal gave part-time employees, over half the company's unionized work force, a 26 percent raise, to at least $21 per hour. Ms. Carlisle makes close to $24 an hour and works around 20 hours a week, giving her time to run a bakery on the side, she said. Her friends who have full-time driving jobs at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  make around $19 an hour, she said.\par \par \uc2 As big as the raises at \uc2 UPS\uc2  were, they did not increase pay by a lot more than inflation. The top wage rate immediately after the latest deal, $44.25 per hour, was 22 percent higher than five years earlier. Over that period, consumer prices rose 21 percent.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 UPS\uc2  typically hires new union workers into part-time jobs, which they hold for a few years. As a result, some people may be unwilling to seek a job at the company.\par \par \uc2 Even so, last year's \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  contract was widely discussed online, spawning memes of \uc2 UPS\uc2  drivers arriving at customers' doors in designer clothing.\par \par \uc2 ''Joking or not, everybody was like, 'Hey, man, I need a job at \uc2 UPS\uc2 ,''' said Juan Martinez, a \uc2 UPS\uc2  driver in Southern California.\par \par \uc2 Under the new contract, Mr. Martinez expects to earn $110,000 to $120,000 a year, depending on how much overtime he gets, he said. He said his income allowed him to spend more on his children's education.\par \par \uc2 Under the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' deal with \uc2 UPS\uc2 , the top hourly wage will increase to $49 by the end of the five-year contract. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said in January that the average pay of workers at its delivery contractors was $20.50 in the United States. \uc2 FedEx\uc2  declined to provide an average wage rate for its delivery workers.\par \par \uc2 Despite \uc2 UPS\uc2 's superior pay over the years, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have not made many inroads at \uc2 FedEx\uc2  or \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The high turnover of delivery and warehouse workers at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  -- where each part-time position was on average filled and vacated twice last year -- makes it difficult to organize them.\par \par \uc2 Another challenge is that delivery workers at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , and drivers who deliver for \uc2 FedEx Ground\uc2 , are employed by contractors. Mr. Rosenfeld, the labor academic, said trying to organize a few dozen people at each contractor could be time consuming and costly.\par \par \uc2 Last year, 84 workers at an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  contractor near Los Angeles joined the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . But days earlier, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  terminated the contract with the operator, Battle-Tested Strategies, the company said, for failing to follow proper safety procedures, among other things.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  asked the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  to rule that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  was a joint employer of the workers and order the company to reinstate the contract. The board has yet to rule.\par \par \uc2 A favorable ruling would be ''a massive deal'' and an ''inspiration for thousands of other workers across the country,'' said Randy Korgan, a \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  official.\par \par \uc2 Johnathon Ervin, the owner of Battle-Tested Strategies, said he believed \uc2 Amazon\uc2  had terminated the contract, which led to job losses for all his employees, because of the unionization effort. An \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokeswoman, Mary Kate Paradis, disputed that.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ervin said the minimum pay for his workers under the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  contract was $19.75. ''If you are asking people to make this a career, you should have better work conditions and pay the drivers more,'' said Mr. Ervin, a 26-year Air Force veteran.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  didn't respond directly to that criticism. The company noted that its contractors, which it refers to as delivery service partners, had created 279,000 driving jobs over the last five years.\par \par \uc2 ''Helping D.S.P.s create a good overall working experience is important to us, which is why we've invested more than $8 billion in state-of-the-art technology, safety features, rates, programs and services for Amazon D.S.P.s and their drivers,'' Ms. Paradis said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Labor groups have made some gains at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , including organizing workers at a Staten Island warehouse. But \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is challenging the election there, and that union has been embroiled in infighting.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 FedEx\uc2  there is another potential barrier to unionization.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  was founded as an airline, and employees of its Express business come under the Railway Labor Act, which requires unions to organize nationally, across a whole company, at once. Union officials say it's easier to hold individual votes at each company location as allowed under the National Labor Relations Act, which governs workers at \uc2 UPS\uc2  and automakers.\par \par \uc2 Even so, some \uc2 FedEx\uc2  employees do belong to a union. Nearly 6,000 pilots at \uc2 FedEx Express\uc2  are represented by the \uc2 Air Line Pilots Association\uc2 . The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  are trying to organize the mechanics who work on the company's planes.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  said its delivery workers had benefited from not being in a union because the company raised wages significantly during the home-delivery boom of 2021 and 2022, when \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers' raises were set by an agreement reached before the pandemic. A \uc2 FedEx\uc2  spokeswoman noted that the company incurred an additional $1.4 billion in labor costs in its 2022 fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 ''We make much more money than anyone else in the industry,'' said Essence Carlisle, a part-time package handler at UPS in Louisville, Ky. (PHOTOGRAPH BY WILLIAM WIDMER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B6) This article appeared in print on page B1, B6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240530ek5u00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
