\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 U.A.W. Asks Officials to Redo Union Vote at Alabama Plants\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Ewing \par \uc2 396 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The union, which lost an organizing vote at two factories last week, argued to federal officials that the automaker had violated labor laws.\par \par \uc2 A week after losing a hard-fought election at two \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  factories in Alabama, the \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  asked federal officials on Friday to order a new vote, saying the German carmaker violated labor laws to suppress support for the union.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  conducted a ''relentless anti-union campaign'' marked by ''wanton lawlessness,'' the U.A.W. said in a complaint to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 . Among other things, the union said, Mercedes fired four employees who supported the union, prevented pro-union employees from campaigning and forced employees to watch anti-union videos.\par \par \uc2 Workers at the Mercedes factories outside Tuscaloosa, which manufacture sport utility vehicles and battery packs, voted 56 percent to 44 percent against joining the union. But the labor board can order a new election if, after a hearing, a regional director determines that improper conduct by an employer affected the vote, a spokeswoman for the board said.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes denied that it had used improper methods to defeat the union drive. A majority of workers ''indicated they are not interested in being represented by the U.A.W.,'' the company said in a statement on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ''Throughout the election, we worked with the N.L.R.B. to adhere to its guidelines, and we will continue to do so,'' Mercedes said.\par \par \uc2 The Alabama result interrupted a string of victories by the U.A.W. in the South, including persuading a large majority of the workers at a \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  factory in Chattanooga, Tenn., to vote to join the union and securing substantial pay raises in a new contract with \uc2 Daimler Truck\uc2  in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Organizing workers in Southern states, which have long been hostile to unions, is a high priority for the U.A.W. The region is attracting a large share of the billions of dollars that companies are investing in electric-car and battery factories.\par \par \uc2 By the same token, Southern elected leaders like Alabama's governor, \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2 , a Republican, have worked to keep unions out, seeing them as a threat to their ability to attract more factories and jobs.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240525ek5p0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
