\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 News Corp\uc2  and \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  Strike a Licensing Dea\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Robertson \par \uc2 460 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The deal gives \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's chatbots access to new and archived material from The Wall Street Journal, The New York Post, MarketWatch and Barron's, among others.\par \par \uc2 News Corp\uc2 , the Murdoch-owned empire of publications like The Wall Street Journal and The New York Post, announced on Wednesday that it had agreed to a deal with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  to share its content to train and service artificial intelligence chatbots.\par \par \uc2 News Corp\uc2  said the multiyear agreement would allow \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  to use current and archived news content from \uc2 News Corp\uc2 's major news outlets, including brands in the United States, United Kingdom and Australia as well as MarketWatch and Barron's. The agreement does not include content from \uc2 News Corp\uc2 's other businesses, such as its digital real estate services or \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We believe an historic agreement will set new standards for veracity, for virtue and for value in the digital age,'' \uc2 Robert Thomson\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 News Corp\uc2 , said in a statement. He described \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and its chief executive, \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2 , as ''principled partners'' who ''understand the commercial and social significance of journalists and journalism.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Altman described the partnership as ''a proud moment for journalism and technology.''\par \par \uc2 The financial terms of the deal were not disclosed. The Wall Street Journal reported the agreement could be worth as much as $250 million over five years, citing unnamed sources. A \uc2 News Corp\uc2  spokesman declined to comment on the reporting.\par \par \uc2 Tim Martell, a spokesman for the Independent Association of Publishers' Employees, which represents workers at The Journal, said in a statement that the union was concerned that it had not reached an agreement on A.I. protections in its current contract negotiations before the \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  deal was announced.\par \par \uc2 Many publishers have worried about the threat to their business posed by generative A.I., which uses copyrighted content to train its models and service its chatbots. The use of A.I. to answer online search queries in particular has raised concerns that publishers are not being compensated for the use of their content to train chatbots that compete with them as a source of information.\par \par \uc2 In December, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  sued \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  for copyright infringement, accusing the tech companies of using millions of its news articles without authorization. \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  have sought to dismiss parts of the complaint. In April, eight daily newspapers owned by \uc2 Alden Global Capital\uc2  filed a similar lawsuit against \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Other publishers have sought to negotiate deals with the tech companies. \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  has already reached agreements with the German publishing giant \uc2 Axel Springer\uc2 , \uc2 Dotdash Meredith\uc2 , The Financial Times and \uc2 The Associated Press\uc2 , among others.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240523ek5n0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
