\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 U.A.W. Loss At Mercedes Was a Blow\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette \par \uc2 1,544 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 After Mercedes workers voted against joining the \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2 , the union will have less momentum as it campaigns to organize Southern factories.\par \par \uc2 After suffering a setback at two \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  plants in Alabama on Friday, the \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2 's efforts to organize other auto factories in the South are likely to slow and could struggle to make headway.\par \par \uc2 About 56 percent of the Mercedes workers who voted rejected the U.A.W. in an election after the union chalked up two major wins this year. In April, workers at a \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  plant in Tennessee voted to join the union, the first large nonunion auto plant in the South to do so. Weeks later, the union negotiated a new contract bringing significant pay and benefit improvements for its members at several North Carolina factories owned by \uc2 Daimler Truck\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Losing at Mercedes is not death for the union,'' said Arthur Wheaton, director of labor studies at Cornell University School of Industrial and Labor Relations. ''It just means they'll have less confidence going to the next plant. The U.A.W. is in it for the long run. I don't think they're going to stop just because they lost here.''\par \par \uc2 Since its founding in 1935, the U.A.W. has almost exclusively represented workers employed by the three Michigan-based automakers: \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , and Chrysler, now part of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . And it has long struggled to make headway at plants owned by foreign manufacturers, especially in Southern states where anti-union sentiment runs deep.\par \par \uc2 Workers at the \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  plant had voted against being represented by the U.A.W. twice by narrow margins before the recent union win there. An effort a decade ago to organize one of the Mercedes plants failed to build enough support for an election.\par \par \uc2 Harley Shaiken\uc2 , a professor emeritus at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , noted that broad union organizing efforts seldom proceeded smoothly. In the 1930s, the U.A.W. won recognition at G.M. and Chrysler but struggled at Ford, which continued employing nonunion workers for a few years.\par \par \uc2 ''I have no doubt they will continue organizing and eventually try for another vote,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In its past efforts in the South, the union was hampered by a negative image, which may have also played a part in the U.A.W.'s loss at Mercedes. For years, the three Michigan automakers were cutting jobs and closing plants, in part because of rigid and costly labor contracts. The union was also hurt by corruption cases that put several former senior officials, including two former U.A.W. presidents, behind bars.\par \par \uc2 Business leaders in Alabama ran a campaign against the U.A.W. that was based in part on the contention that the union was responsible for the decline of Detroit. In a January opinion essay published in The Alabama Daily News, the chief executive of the Business Council of Alabama, Helena Duncan, said the state would suffer the same fate if workers voted for the union.\par \par \uc2 ''Much of the decay that exists in the 'Motor City' today results from untenable demands that the U.A.W. placed on its automobile manufacturers, an unwise move that sent untold numbers of jobs to right-to-work states like ours and crippled a once great metropolis,'' Ms. Duncan wrote.\par \par \uc2 A year ago, the union elected a new president, Shawn Fain, who was untouched by the corruption scandals and vowed to take a more aggressive approach in contract talks. Then last fall, the union came away with substantial pay and benefit gains in negotiations with the Detroit automakers, after targeted strikes over some 40 days. Hundreds of Southern autoworkers began reaching out, asking for help organizing their nonunion plants. The U.A.W. responded by announcing that it would spend $40 million on organizing drives over the next two years.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not scared at all,'' Mr. Fain said Friday in Alabama after the union lost the Mercedes vote. ''I believe workers want unions, I believe they want justice, and we're going to continue doing what we can do.''\par \par \uc2 Mercedes in a statement emphasized its direct relationship with workers and said it looked forward to making sure the company was ''not only their employer of choice, but a place they would recommend to friends and family.''\par \par \uc2 The union has signaled that it expects to focus its organizing efforts on another Alabama plant -- a Hyundai factory in Montgomery. But organizing that plant will probably be even harder than the campaign at the Mercedes factories, said Erik Gordon, a \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  business professor who follows the auto industry.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. had allies at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and Mercedes. Unions are powerful players in Germany, where those two companies are based. Under German law, worker representatives must occupy half the seats on a company's supervisory board, the equivalent of an American board of directors.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and Mercedes both have groups called works councils through which managers and employees discuss and negotiate workplace issues and production plans. In its drive at the \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  plant in Chattanooga, the U.A.W. had the support of the company's works council and \uc2 IG Metall\uc2 , the powerful union that represents all German automotive workers.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. won't have that kind of support at Hyundai's Montgomery plant, Mr. Gordon said. ''In general, Korean car companies have more adversarial relationships with unions than do the German manufacturers,'' he said. ''Korean companies are less used to sitting together in a conference room with unions.''\par \par \uc2 Last year, weeks after the U.A.W. won pay and benefit increases from the three Michigan-based automakers, Hyundai announced that it would increase its workers' pay sharply over the next four years -- a move widely seen as an attempt to dampen workers' interest in joining the U.A.W.\par \par \uc2 ''The decision to be represented by a union is up to our team members,'' Hyundai said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The Montgomery plant makes two popular sport utility vehicles -- the Tucson and Santa Fe -- and employs about 4,000 workers. An earlier U.A.W. drive to organize the plant in 2016 petered out without coming to a vote.\par \par \uc2 Last fall, the union said it planned to target plants owned by 10 foreign-owned automakers -- \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2 , Hyundai, \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , \uc2 BMW\uc2 , Mercedes, \uc2 Subaru\uc2 , \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , \uc2 Mazda\uc2  and Volvo -- and others owned by \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , which is based in Texas, and two smaller electric vehicle start-ups, \uc2 Lucid\uc2  and \uc2 Rivian\uc2 , both based in California.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. plants owned by those foreign and U.S. companies employ nearly 150,000 workers in 13 states, the union said.\par \par \uc2 In Alabama, however, the U.A.W. faced perhaps a more hostile environment than anywhere else. While it was campaigning at Mercedes, Gov. \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2  spoke out against the union and headed a group of six Southern governors, all Republicans, who issued a letter suggesting unionizing could cause automakers to move jobs out of their states. One senior Alabama politician described the U.A.W. as ''leeches.''\par \par \uc2 Mercedes brought in Nick Saban, the hugely popular former football coach at the University of Alabama, to talk to workers in an effort to persuade them to vote against the U.A.W.\par \par \uc2 Unions are traditionally seen as a Northern institution and are often linked with the civil rights movement, which alienates many people in Alabama, Mr. Gordon said. ''It's a very tough place for the U.A.W.,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 That antipathy could also make it hard for the U.A.W. to negotiate contracts guaranteeing its members raises and other gains even if it wins unionizing votes. Lawmakers who oppose unions may put pressure on employers not to make big concessions in negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fain and the U.A.W. have argued that unions are the best way for workers to demand higher wages when automakers are enjoying strong sales and profits in North America.\par \par \uc2 Public support of unions is stronger than it has been in years, including in the South. This year, 600 workers at an electric bus factory in Alabama voted to join the \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2  union. A week ago, they negotiated a new contract delivering pay raises and enhanced benefits.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. and other unions also have enjoyed the support of President Biden, who last fall joined striking autoworkers on a picket line in Michigan. The union endorsed Mr. Biden in this year's election.\par \par \uc2 But that close association with the president may also hurt the U.A.W. with conservative workers in a Southern state who prefer Mr. Biden's opponent -- former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 . Mr. Fain and Mr. Trump have often criticized each other, but polls have shown that a sizable minority of union households support the former president.\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain, above left, head of the U.A.W., at a news conference Friday in union headquarters in Tuscaloosa, Ala., left. ''I believe workers want unions,'' Mr. Fain said. ''I believe they want justice.'' (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KIM CHANDLER/ASSOCIATED PRESS; CHARITY RACHELLE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B1, B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240520ek5k0003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
