\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Turning Point As Auto Union Eyes Alabama\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Ewing \par \uc2 1,496 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Southern political leaders say a win for the \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  would threaten their economies. Activists want to strike a blow against a system they say exploits the poor.\par \par \uc2 More than 5,000 \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  workers in Alabama are voting this week on whether to join the \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2 , a decision both supporters and opponents say will have consequences far beyond two factories near Tuscaloosa where the German carmaker churns out luxury sport utility vehicles and batteries for electric cars.\par \par \uc2 Conservative political leaders have portrayed the union campaign to organize Mercedes workers as an assault by outsiders on the region's economy and way of life. The vote tally is expected to be released by federal officials on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Six Southern governors, including \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2 , an Alabama Republican, issued a statement last month criticizing unions as ''special interests looking to come into our state and threaten our jobs and the values we live by.'' Alabama recently passed a law intended to discourage union organizing.\par \par \uc2 For the union, a win would add to a string of victories in the South, where organized labor has traditionally been weak, and provide momentum to the U.A.W.'s efforts to win over workers at other nonunion automakers like Hyundai, \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2  and \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If the U.A.W. loses, it could sharply slow down a campaign by the union's president, Shawn Fain, to organize auto and battery plants across the country. That effort began after the union last fall reached new contracts with hefty pay raises and other benefits for workers at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent company of Chrysler, \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram.\par \par \uc2 In Alabama, which was a crucible of the civil rights movement, union organizers and supporters cast the Mercedes campaign as part of a decades-long struggle to dismantle an economic system based on exploitation of poor people.\par \par \uc2 ''You are not just fighting for a union,'' Bishop William Barber II, an activist and professor at the Yale Divinity School, told a group of organizers, workers and supporters at a Montgomery church on Monday. ''You are fighting for justice.''\par \par \uc2 U.A.W. supporters were optimistic as workers cast their ballots at a Mercedes car factory in Vance, Ala., and at a company-owned factory in nearby Woodstock that assembles battery packs for electric vehicles. The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  is overseeing the weeklong polling.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel like we have the upper hand right now,'' said Sammie Ellis, a union organizer who installs wiring in Mercedes vehicles. He spoke outside a cluttered storefront office down the road from the factory in Vance where activists seated on folding chairs plotted strategy amid piles of placards with slogans like ''Mercedes Workers United'' and ''End the Alabama Discount.''\par \par \uc2 The Alabama discount is a reference to what union activists say is the state's main attraction to investors: low wages and compliant workers. ''They come to take advantage of how Alabama workers are living in poorer conditions than workers in other parts of the country,'' said Joe Cleveland, an official with an \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  local in Anniston, Ala.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes said in a statement that the company ''has a proven record of competitively compensating team members and providing many additional benefits.''\par \par \uc2 Workers who have been at Mercedes for four years can earn $34 an hour, and some employees say they are grateful for the way the company has treated them.\par \par \uc2 ''Mercedes has done a lot for me,'' Yolanda Berry, a team leader at the carmaker, said in a video posted on X by Autos Drive America, an industry association that represents Mercedes and other foreign automakers with plants in the United States. Ms. Berry said she had earned less than $14 an hour at a previous job.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. is on a roll in the South after workers at a \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  factory in Chattanooga, Tenn., voted in April to be represented by the union. Also that month, the union won significant pay raises for \uc2 Daimler Truck\uc2  workers in North Carolina. A victory at Mercedes, which became a separate company from \uc2 Daimler Truck\uc2  in 2021, would bolster the union in its next campaign, organizing workers at a Hyundai factory in Montgomery, about 100 miles south of Tuscaloosa.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean company produces S.U.V.s at the Montgomery plant, including the Tucson and Santa Fe models. Union organizers are also targeting a \uc2 Honda\uc2  factory in Lincoln, Ala., where the Japanese company makes S.U.V.s and pickups. But that effort is in its early stages.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, about 50 activists and Hyundai workers gathered at Immanuel Presbyterian Church in Montgomery to sing union fight songs and hear from Bishop Barber.\par \par \uc2 Paraphrasing the Rev. Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr.\uc2 , Bishop Barber accused Southern political leaders of pitting races against each other. They fear Blacks ''and poor whites uniting together and forming a voting bloc that does fundamentally reshape the economic architecture of the country and of the state,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Opposition to the union from Alabama's Republican political leadership has been intense. After likening the U.A.W. to ''leeches,'' Nathaniel Ledbetter, the Republican speaker of the Alabama House of Representatives, helped push through a law that denies state funding to companies that voluntarily recognize unions.\par \par \uc2 The law will not directly affect the Mercedes vote, but it reflected the state of alarm among Republicans with close ties to business interests and their determination to stop union advances. Ms. Ivey signed the bill into law on Monday.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Ms. Ivey declined a request for an interview, referring to public statements she has made on the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''Unionization would certainly put our states' jobs in jeopardy,'' Ms. Ivey said in a statement she issued with the governors of Georgia, Mississippi, South Carolina, Tennessee and Texas, all Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ledbetter's office did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 A union drive at the Hyundai factory in Alabama in 2016 failed, but activists say things have changed. ''The first time around, people were easily intimidated and scared by anti-union tactics,'' said Quichelle Liggins, who has worked at the Hyundai factory for 12 years. ''This time, we're ready.''\par \par \uc2 In an apparent effort to blunt the appeal of a union, Hyundai was one of several automakers that raised worker pay after the U.A.W. won gains for members at Ford, G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The raises at Hyundai, announced in November, amounted to 14 percent over the previous year, according to the company.\par \par \uc2 But pay is not the only issue for many autoworkers in Alabama. Ms. Liggins, a single mother of two, said she hoped a union would protect people like her from long hours and unpredictable work schedules. ''I had a manager tell me my job was more important than my family,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Hyundai said, ''We are deeply committed to supporting quality jobs that pay competitive salaries and offer industry-leading benefits.''\par \par \uc2 The company said that, with rare exceptions, it gave employees 30 days of notice about changes to their schedules. Employees are not required to work more than 10 hours a day, Hyundai said in a statement, and overtime is voluntary except during the introduction of a new model when repair and quality control teams may be required to work longer.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes, based in Stuttgart, Germany, is used to dealing with unions in its home country, where by law half the members of the company's supervisory board represent employees. But in Alabama the company has opposed the union campaign. The U.A.W. has even accused the company of using illegal tactics.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. has filed six charges of unfair labor practices against Mercedes with the labor relations board, saying the company disciplined employees for discussing unionization at work, prevented organizers from distributing union materials, conducted surveillance of workers and fired workers who supported the union.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes denies the claims. The company ''has not interfered with or retaliated against any team member in their right to pursue union representation,'' it said in a statement, adding that it ''firmly denies it has made any adverse employment decision based on union affiliation.''\par \par \uc2 Mercedes has also raised pay in recent months and made an effort to give workers more notice about changes in their schedules, workers said. But Mr. Ellis, the activist, said the improvements had come only ''because of the union knocking at the door.''\par \par \uc2 At a U.A.W. organizing event in Birmingham, Ala. (A1); Quichelle Liggins, who has worked at Hyundai for 12 years, said she hoped a union would block unpredictable work schedules. Sammie Ellis, a U.A.W. organizer and autoworker, said Mercedes had improved pay and benefits only because of the U.A.W.'s efforts. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY CHARITY RACHELLE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A20) This article appeared in print on page A1, A20. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240517ek5h0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{