\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Daimler Truck\uc2  Deal Averts Strike in North Carolina\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Ewing \par \uc2 646 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  reached an agreement involving workers who make \uc2 Freightliner\uc2  trucks and Thomas Built buses. The deal comes as the union seeks to expand in Southern states.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  reached an 11th-hour deal on Friday with \uc2 Daimler Truck\uc2  in North Carolina that gave workers 25 percent raises over the next four years and averted a strike that would have begun Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The union had said it was ready to walk out if it could not agree on a new contract covering 7,300 \uc2 Daimler\uc2  employees. The previous contract expired on Friday. The German company has four factories in North Carolina, where it builds Freightliner and Western Star trucks, and Thomas Built buses. The union also represents workers at parts distribution centers in Atlanta and Memphis.\par \par \uc2 The deal, which includes profit sharing, automatic cost-of-living increases and equalizes pay among workers at the North Carolina factories, marks a victory for the U.A.W. as it tries to expand its power in Southern states where unions have long been weak.\par \par \uc2 ''When that deadline came closer, the company suddenly became ready to talk,'' Shawn Fain, the U.A.W.'s leader, said late Friday as he announced the agreement, which will give workers raises of at least 16 percent the first year after they ratify the contract.\par \par \uc2 Daimler Trucks said in a statement that it hoped U.A.W. members would approve the tentative contracts, ''for the mutual benefit of all parties.''\par \par \uc2 A walkout would have had national political repercussions. North Carolina is a political battleground state that has a Democratic governor, but President Biden narrowly lost the state in 2020. Mr. Biden had indicated that he could step in aggressively to support the \uc2 Daimler\uc2  workers, possibly putting him at odds with the state's more pro-business Democrats just months before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. has been making inroads in the South. It scored a significant victory this month when workers at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's factory in Chattanooga, Tenn., voted to be represented by the union. Workers at a \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  factory in Alabama will vote on whether to unionize in mid-May.\par \par \uc2 Workers at \uc2 Daimler Truck\uc2 , which split from \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  in 2021, have been represented by the U.A.W. for several decades. The union has adopted a more assertive stance after winning the biggest pay increases in decades for workers at \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the owner of \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , Chrysler and Ram after strikes at the three companies last year.\par \par \uc2 The gains for workers at \uc2 Daimler\uc2  could add momentum to the U.A.W.'s drive to organize U.S. auto factories, including at companies like \uc2 Toyota\uc2  and \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Workers in North Carolina said they have been struggling to make ends meet while \uc2 Daimler\uc2  racked up big profits. The company, based in Stuttgart, reported net profit last year of 4 billion euros, or $4.25 billion, a 44 percent increase from a year earlier. Sales in the United States, Canada and Mexico generated more than half the profit.\par \par \uc2 The union points out that Thomas Built, whose yellow school buses are a common sight, has benefited from millions of dollars in federal subsidies for electric buses. Thomas Built workers have earned less than counterparts at other factories, but the deal will give them pay increases to make up the deficit.\par \par \uc2 ''Workers who make trucks and workers who make buses will get equal pay for equal work,'' Mr. Fain said.\par \par \uc2 The agreement also includes provisions aimed at preserving jobs in North Carolina. Workers had sought more job security after the company moved some production to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain, U.A.W. president, announced the agreement covering 7,300 workers late Friday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GEORGE WALKER/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240428ek4s0004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
