\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Impasse Over, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  And Union Resume Talks\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. Edward Moreno \par \uc2 621 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The company and Workers United, which represents more than 10,000 of the chain's employees, broke off negotiation nearly a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and the union that represents over 10,000 of its workers returned to the bargaining table on Wednesday for the first time in nearly a year, a pivotal moment in the yearslong battle between the coffee giant and its organized workers.\par \par \uc2 Company representatives and 150 representatives from the union, Workers United, met at an undisclosed location in Atlanta to begin negotiating a framework for union contracts for each of the over 400 unionized stores.\par \par \uc2 The last time the two sides sat down was May 23, and they have spent months blaming each other for the impasse. During that time, workers have staged several strikes and tried to win seats on \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's board, and the company has sued the union over its use of the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  logo.\par \par \uc2 The feuding eased in February when the two sides issued a joint statement saying they were returning to the bargaining table. Michelle Eisen, a longtime barista at a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in Buffalo that was the first company-owned store to unionize during the current campaign, said she was optimistic that the company would bargain in good faith.\par \par \uc2 ''It's been a long couple of years, and it feels like there's some levity now and a little more lightness in general,'' Ms. Eisen said. ''All signs point in a positive direction.''\par \par \uc2 Reflecting the new attitude, over 250 union members plan to attend Wednesday's session virtually, a way for workers to ensure that all voices are heard. Last year, the union said, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  insisted that the talks be held completely in person.\par \par \uc2 The union is asking for higher wages and better safety standards, among other issues. Once the two sides agree on an overall framework, individual contracts will be put up to ratification votes by each store. The separate contracts will allow the company and workers to raise issues that may vary by region or type of store, like one that has a drive-through window versus one at a mall.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers began organizing in 2021 with three Buffalo-area stores. Since that campaign began, the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  has filed numerous complaints accusing \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  of taking steps to resist organizing efforts, which the company has denied.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and the union announced that they would return to the bargaining table, the company said it would provide unionized workers with benefits it introduced in 2022 but withheld from union stores, including credit card tipping.\par \par \uc2 ''The union forced management to the bargaining table,'' said Eric Blanc, a professor at \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2  who studies labor movements. ''The scale of the union-busting campaign and its ferocity is unmatched in modern labor history. The fact that the workers were able to overcome that is truly historic.''\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  became willing to resume talks with the union nearly a year after \uc2 Laxman Narasimhan\uc2  arrived as chief executive. He took over from the longtime chief executive \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , who has said a union was incompatible with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's business model.\par \par \uc2 In March, a coalition of labor unions including Workers United ended its campaign to add its members to the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  board, saying in a statement that it was ''time to acknowledge the progress that has been made and to allow the company and its workers to focus on moving forward.''\par \par \uc2 The union alliance also said it had a ''meaningful dialogue'' with shareholders who said they believed the company was moving to improve its relationship with its workers.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY JOSHUA BESSEX/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240426ek4p0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
