\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Justices Receptive To \uc2 Starbucks\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. Edward Moreno \par \uc2 821 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The case seeks to limit the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 's ability to obtain court intervention in labor cases.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court justices on Tuesday appeared willing to side with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in the company's challenge to a federal judge's order to reinstate workers who were attempting to unionize a store in Memphis.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has asked the court to make it harder for the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  to obtain intervention by judges in cases where a company is accused of violating labor law. The case stems from the February 2022 firing of seven workers who let local journalists into a closed store to conduct interviews about their unionization efforts.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  prevails, it would be more difficult for workers to be reinstated if they're fired during a labor dispute. The case is being heard a day before \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is set to return to the bargaining table with the union that represents about 10,000 of its workers after a contentious, monthslong impasse.\par \par \uc2 A majority of the justices seemed sympathetic to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ' argument for a more rigorous test for allowing the labor board to reinstate workers. Through their questioning, the justices appeared to see the looser standard that the N.L.R.B. was given in this case as an outlier.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , which has faced hundreds of accusations of labor law violations across the country, argued that there is a patchwork of standards under which the N.L.R.B. can seek a court injunction. The appellate court in this case, the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit, applies a lower standard, and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  argued on Tuesday for a stricter, uniform standard nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The standard used by the Sixth Circuit considers two factors. Other circuits use a four-factor test, which requires that the N.L.R.B. prove the possibility of irreparable harm if the injunction to reinstate workers isn't granted, among other things.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  pointed out that other federal agencies must abide by the four-part test to obtain an injunction. ''So why is this particular statutory regime different than so many others?'' he asked.\par \par \uc2 Austin Raynor, a Justice Department lawyer arguing for the labor board, said that the board had asked for injunctions sparingly and only in particularly egregious cases. The N.L.R.B. has requested injunctions about 15 times in the past year, out of hundreds of complaints it filed.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  pointed to that when questioning \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ' lawyer, appearing to ask why the court needed to intervene. ''This is not sounding like a huge problem,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Bodie, a professor at the University of Minnesota Law School, said that he expected a mild ''housekeeping'' opinion focused on bringing clarity and uniformity across circuits rather than a ruling on the labor board's authority.\par \par \uc2 He said that injunctions were an important tool for the labor board. Without the ability to reinstate workers while proceedings play out, he said, employers could intimidate workers and chip away at union-supporting ranks.\par \par \uc2 ''It dampens the whole process, and it's more likely to result in a union loss,'' said Mr. Bodie, a former N.L.R.B. attorney.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said the workers were fired because admitting the journalists into the store violated several company policies. Starbucks Workers United, the union representing the company's workers, filed an unfair labor practice charge over the firings, arguing that the company selectively enforced the rules against organized workers. The labor board issued a complaint against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  two months later.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge granted the labor board's request to reinstate the workers while proceedings over the firings played out, which could take years. An appellate judge upheld the reinstatements last year, and the company requested the Supreme Court review. The high court agreed to hear the case in January.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Blatt, a partner at the law firm \uc2 Williams & Connolly\uc2 , is representing \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and is a veteran of the Supreme Court bar. In the past two years, she won rulings in favor of \uc2 Google\uc2  -- a closely watched case seeking to make tech companies liable for content posted by its users -- and for Jack Daniel's in an intellectual property case against the seller of a dog toy.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth B. Prelogar, the U.S. solicitor general, is representing the labor board. Mr. Raynor, the assistant solicitor general, argued the case on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers began organizing in 2021 with three Buffalo-area stores. Now, the union represents about 10,000 workers across more than 400 corporate-owned stores.\par \par \uc2 Several companies led by \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  have challenged the constitutionality of the N.L.R.B. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has sought to distance itself from that litigation.\par \par \uc2 These Memphis Starbucks workers were fired after the company said they violated safety and security policies. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ADRIAN SAINZ/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240424ek4o0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
