\par\par \uc2 READER COMMENTS\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTMB\par \b \uc2 Requiem for a Hamptons Day Laborer\b0\par\par\uc2 722 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Requiem for a Hamptons Day Laborer\par \par \uc2 Readers responded by letter and at nytimes.com to Ginia Bellafante's April 7 article about the death of a man who lived a perilous life on Long Island. Comments were edited.\par \par \uc2 How many more distressing accounts must we endure before our state takes decisive action? The Unemployment Bridge Program was ostensibly designed to provide assistance to day laborers and other vulnerable worker demographics during lean periods, offering a lifeline that spares them from perilous conditions and compromised health. Yet, the issue of wage theft, rampant among undocumented laborers, continues to plague our communities. The money allocated by our state to combat this exploitation remains woefully inadequate. How can workers evade homelessness and sustain themselves without a safety net? Further, as rent costs soar and housing options dwindle, how can low-wage earners keep pace with the escalating cost of living? These challenges compound the plight of immigrant and low-income workers and their families, perpetuating a cycle of hardship and despair. We must unite to prioritize human lives over profit margins. By pooling our collective intellect and resources, we can compel our government to implement systems that prioritize the most vulnerable and in need.\par \par \uc2 Janet Rolon-Fry, Ossining, N.Y.\par \par \uc2 The writer is deputy executive director of the Community Resource Center, an organization based in Mamaroneck, N.Y., that helps new immigrants in Westchester County.\par \par \uc2 It costs an arm and a leg to get work done in the Hamptons. The rich pay a lot. The problem is that the money doesn't reach the laborers. This happens throughout the United States: From cleaning services to delivery services, the middle man keeps the money, and the lowest man on the totem pole is exploited.\par \par \uc2 Janet Liff, New York\par \par \uc2 Infuriating that people willingly benefit from service workers, and then erect barriers to affordable housing for them. The fact that this has persisted for decades suggests that a critical mass of people there think this situation is acceptable.\par \par \uc2 Justin Ginnetti, Geneva\par \par \uc2 This story exemplifies why immigration never gets fixed. No administration has or will ever shut down the border and deport all undocumented immigrants as Trump boasts he will do, because it would crash the economy. Thirty percent of construction is done by immigrants, a large portion of whom are undoubtedly undocumented. Without them housing costs and shortages would get even worse, and grocery prices would soar. Homeowners who need landscaping and repairs, and not just the wealthy but middle-class suburbanites, turn a blind eye to it. The next time you need your roof repaired, it will most likely be done by immigrants. Instead everybody has walrus tears for these exploited workers, with no intention of ever fixing it.\par \par \uc2 Philip Loubere, Murfreesboro, Tenn.\par \par \uc2 A sad story in one of the wealthiest countries in the world, and one of the wealthiest sections of the country. There for the grace of God go I, and I imagine many of The Times's readers.\par \par \uc2 Doug Ritter, Rome\par \par \uc2 In Casablanca, where I grew up, all of the household hands from the maids, the cook, the gardener and the house guard lived in the guesthouse on the property, no one had to go fend for themselves in the housing market. Granted, they didn't get paid that much, but they lived in dignity, had the right for a one-month vacation for a religious holiday and were always welcomed back to their work/home.\par \par \uc2 Hisham Oumlil, Brooklyn\par \par \uc2 I live in Suffolk County, and unfortunately this problem is not confined to the Hamptons. We have immigrant day laborers living in the woods in places like Huntington, too. There is a severe shortage of affordable housing, and the laborers are not eligible for county-run shelters because most of them are undocumented. The Family Service League in Huntington coordinates a program involving local houses of worship to provide shelter and food for the men during the winter months. It's a tragic situation that we have people living in the woods amid so much wealth.\par \par \uc2 Sandra Levine, Babylon, N.Y.\par \par \uc2 The writer serves as director of grants funding for the Family Service League.\par \par \uc2 Julio Florencio Teo Gomez, an immigrant laborer, died after being hit by a car. This article appeared in print on page MB3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240421ek4l00064\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
