\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Google\uc2  Fires 28 Employees Involved in Protest of Israeli Cloud Contract\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nico Grant \par \uc2 428 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The dismissals escalated longstanding tensions between company leaders and activist employees opposed to supplying technology to Israel's government.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  fired 28 workers on Wednesday after dozens of employees participated in sit-ins at the company's New York and Sunnyvale, Calif., offices to protest the company's cloud computing contract with the Israeli government.\par \par \uc2 A day earlier, nine employees were arrested on charges of trespassing at the two offices.\par \par \uc2 ''Physically impeding other employees' work and preventing them from accessing our facilities is a clear violation of our policies, and completely unacceptable behavior,'' a \uc2 Google\uc2  spokeswoman said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Years before the dismissals, tensions had been simmering between the company's management and some activist employees over Project Nimbus, a $1.2 billion \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  deal to supply the Israeli government with cloud services, such as artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 That discord had deepened since the war in Gaza began in October. \uc2 Google\uc2  recently fired an employee who disrupted an Israeli technology conference in New York. And the company is even planning to make changes to a corporate forum because employees were bickering about the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  said it would continue to investigate the Tuesday protests. In Sunnyvale, employees refused to leave the office of \uc2 Thomas Kurian\uc2 , the chief executive of Google Cloud.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  employees affiliated with the group that organized the sit-ins, called No Tech for Apartheid, said in a statement that the firings were ''a flagrant act of retaliation.''\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Google\uc2  workers have the right to peacefully protest about terms and conditions of our labor,'' the employees said. They added that some of the employees \uc2 Google\uc2  fired had not participated in the sit-ins.\par \par \uc2 The Nimbus contract, announced in 2021, was to supply various Israeli government ministries with cloud software. Since the contract's inception, some \uc2 Google\uc2  employees have expressed concern that the company was aiding Israel's military.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Google\uc2  spokeswoman said Nimbus ''is not directed at highly sensitive, classified or military workloads relevant to weapons or intelligence services.''\par \par \uc2 In 2018, \uc2 Google\uc2  workers successfully pushed the company to end a deal with the U.S. Defense Department. Called Project Maven, it would have helped the military analyze drone videos.\par \par \uc2 Employees who have taken part in Nimbus activism said in their statement that they would continue protesting ''until the company drops Project Nimbus.''\par \par \uc2 A protest on Tuesday in a parking lot in Sunnyvale, Calif., near the Google Cloud offices. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Nathan Frandino/Reuters FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240419ek4j0004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
