\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 VW Workers Request Vote To Unionize in Tennessee\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette \par \uc2 949 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  said a ''supermajority'' of the 4,000 eligible workers at a \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  factory had signed cards backing the union.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  employees in Tennessee who are hoping to join the \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  asked a federal agency on Monday to hold an election, a key step toward the union's longtime goal of organizing nonunion factories across the South.\par \par \uc2 The union said \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  workers had filed a petition with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  asking for a unionization vote after a ''supermajority'' of the 4,000 eligible workers at the plant had signed cards supporting the U.A.W.\par \par \uc2 It did not specify how many workers had signed cards, but it previously advised workers to get the support of more than 70 percent of hourly workers and establish a robust organizing committee before seeking an election. A simple majority vote is needed to win representation.\par \par \uc2 ''Today, we are one step closer to making a good job at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  into a great career,'' Isaac Meadows, an assembly worker at the plant, said in a statement issued by the union.\par \par \uc2 An election at the factory would be the first test of the U.A.W.'s strength after the union staged a wave of strikes in the fall against the three Detroit automakers -- \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  -- and winning record wage increases -- with encouragement from the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Monday, \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  said it would ''fully support'' an election giving every employee a chance to vote on union representation. ''We respect our workers' right to a democratic process and to determine who should represent their interests,'' the company said.\par \par \uc2 Later Monday, President Biden issued a statement congratulating the \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  workers for filing for the union election.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. has been hoping to use momentum from its bargaining with the Detroit-based manufacturers to organize nonunion plants in Southern states that pay significantly lower wages than union factories. The U.A.W. says it plans to spend $40 million over the next three years on its campaign.\par \par \uc2 Chattanooga workers have voted on U.A.W. representation twice before, and slim majorities rejected unionization each time. In a 2014 vote, the union had no opposition from \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  management, but there was vocal resistance from state Republican leaders, who suggested that unionizing would jeopardize expansion and job growth at the plant. A second narrow loss came in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Arthur Wheaton, director of labor studies at Cornell University School of Industrial and Labor Relations, said the U.A.W. was in a strong position to gain a majority this time.\par \par \uc2 ''Now they have a lot more support from legislators, the public and the president,'' he said. ''They can say, 'We can help you get better wages and benefits, and we proved we could do that with the Big Three.'''\par \par \uc2 Over the last 40 years, \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2 , Hyundai and other foreign-owned automakers have built more than two dozen auto plants across the South and the lower Midwest, drawn by right-to-work laws that make it harder for unions to organize workers.\par \par \uc2 They also often chose to build factories in rural areas where prevailing wages are significantly lower than in Michigan and other Northern states.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  effort, union campaigns are underway at a \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  plant and a Hyundai factory, both in Alabama. The union says more than half of the Mercedes workers and more than 30 percent of the Hyundai workers have signed cards supporting U.A.W. membership.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  workers said they wanted to join the U.A.W. to push for higher wages, more time off and improved safety measures. The Chattanooga factory opened in 2011 and makes the Atlas full-size sport utility vehicle and the ID.4 electric vehicle. It is the world's only \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  plant without union representation.\par \par \uc2 ''VW has partnered with unionized work forces around the world to make their plants safe and successful,'' Victor Vaughn, a logistics worker, said in the union statement. ''That's why we're voting for a voice at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  here in Chattanooga.''\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. is likely to have support in the election from the powerful German labor union, \uc2 IG Metall\uc2 . Under German law, worker representatives occupy half of the seats on \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 's supervisory board, the equivalent of an American board of directors.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. has sought for years to organize these nonunion auto plants without success, but unions have had a resurgence in recent years in organizing efforts and contract showdowns.\par \par \uc2 G.M., Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  agreed in the fall to roughly 25 percent wage increases for workers making the top U.A.W. wage, and even larger raises for workers farther down on the pay scale.\par \par \uc2 Within a few years, almost all of the 146,000 U.A.W. workers at the Detroit companies will earn more than $40 an hour -- the equivalent of about $83,000 a year for those working 40 hours a week.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  plant announced an 11 percent pay increase shortly after the strikes at the Big Three, bringing the top hourly wage for production workers to $32.40.\par \par \uc2 Nonunion auto plants generally start new workers at less than $20 an hour and pay a top wage under $30 an hour.\par \par \uc2 ''Today, we are one step closer to making a good job at Volkswagen into a great career,'' said Isaac Meadows, an assembly worker at the Volkswagen factory in Chattanooga, Tenn., above. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MELISSA GOLDEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) This article appeared in print on page B1, B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240321ek3j0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
