\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Labor; About 600 Workers Unionize at \uc2 Activision\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Weise \par \uc2 681 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The group will become the largest union at a video game company in the United States, while \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  pledged to stay neutral on the vote.\par \par \uc2 About 600 workers at \uc2 Activision Publishing\uc2 , the video game maker owned by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , are unionizing, forming the largest video game workers' union in the United States, the \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2  said on Friday. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  recognized the union after the vote count was finalized.\par \par \uc2 The employees work in quality assurance, testing \uc2 Activision\uc2 's games for bugs, glitches and other defects, and 390 of them voted to form a union, while eight opposed the effort, the union said. About 200 workers did not vote.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  acquired \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2 , the maker of Call of Duty and other blockbusters, for $69 billion in October. As part of its lengthy effort to convince regulators to approve the deal, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  signed a first-of-its-kind pact in the industry to remain neutral if workers wanted to unionize with the C.W.A.\par \par \uc2 Managers were trained not to express an opinion about whether unionization was good or bad, and the C.W.A. said \uc2 Activision\uc2 's management upheld the pact and did not interfere in the workers' organizing efforts.\par \par \uc2 ''That has been, organizing-wise, a huge blessing,'' said Kara Fannon, a member of the union organizing committee who works for \uc2 Activision\uc2  near Minneapolis. ''It has helped with a lot of people who were concerned about union busting or potential retaliation.''\par \par \uc2 The new union is the first at \uc2 Activision\uc2  since the pact went into effect.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's choice will strengthen its corporate culture and ability to serve its customers and should serve as a model for the industry,'' C.W.A.'s president, \uc2 Claude Cummings Jr.\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The largest group of employees is in Minnesota, though the union also includes offices in Texas and California. The union's hopes include negotiating higher pay, improving job security and providing more advancement opportunities for the quality assurance testers, who perform some of the lowest-paid work in game development.\par \par \uc2 The pact also meant workers were able to sidestep the lengthy and often contentious process of petitioning the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  for an election. Instead, they used an expedited process, where workers indicated their support or opposition for the union by either signing a union authorization card or voting confidentially online in a portal that opened Feb. 22 and closed on Thursday afternoon. A third-party arbiter, Fred Horowitz, verified the results.\par \par \uc2 After the vote count, Amy Pannoni, a deputy general counsel at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , said in a statement that the company looked forward to ''continuing our positive labor management relationship,'' and that it recognized C.W.A. ''as the bargaining representative for the \uc2 Activision Publishing\uc2  central quality assurance employees.''\par \par \uc2 Workers had been organizing at \uc2 Activision\uc2  since at least 2021, when employees across the company staged a walkout after a California civil rights agency sued the company for workplace sexual misconduct. (The company settled the case on narrower grounds last year.) Over time, workers' organizing focused on unionization, with the support of the C.W.A.\par \par \uc2 The workers also saw the successful effort last year to unionize at \uc2 ZeniMax Media\uc2 , a video game company also owned by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , which had expanded its neutrality pact to cover any of the other video game studios it owned. In January 2023, about 300 workers at \uc2 ZeniMax\uc2 , whose Bethesda Game Studios makes hits such as The Elder Scrolls, voted to unionize through the new, expedited process.\par \par \uc2 After Friday's results, the C.W.A. now represents more than 1,000 video game workers at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The video game industry has been roiled by a series of layoffs and other cost-cutting measures for more than a year. Ms. Fannon said that getting better layoff protections, such as improved severance, has been a common concern among workers even before \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  cut 1,900 jobs in its video game division in January, including many layoffs at \uc2 Activision\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240309ek390003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
