\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Joblessness A Quandary In California\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kurtis Lee \par \uc2 1,272 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tech layoffs, fallout from Hollywood strikes and an uptick in rural joblessness challenge a state with one of the nation's highest unemployment rates.\par \par \uc2 For decades, California's behemoth economy has outpaced those of most nations, holding an outsize role in shaping global trends in tech, entertainment and agriculture.\par \par \uc2 While that reputation remains, the state has a less enviable distinction: one of the nation's highest unemployment rates.\par \par \uc2 Nationwide, the rate is 3.7 percent, and in January, the country added 353,000 jobs. California's job growth has been slower than the nationwide average over the last year, and the unemployment rate remains stubbornly high -- 5.1 percent in the latest data, a percentage point higher than a year earlier and outpaced only by Nevada's 5.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 With layoffs in the tech-centered Bay Area, a slow rebound in Southern California from prolonged strikes in the entertainment industry and varying demand for agricultural workers, California is facing economic headwinds in the new year. And residents feel it.\par \par \uc2 The state has historically had higher unemployment than the U.S. average because of a work force that is younger and fast growing, said Sarah Bohn, a senior fellow at the \uc2 Public Policy Institute of California\uc2 . Still, she noted, the labor force shrank in California in the past six months -- a troubling trend.\par \par \uc2 ''When looking at this shrinking, are there less opportunities and people have just stopped looking for work?'' Ms. Bohn asked. ''What will this mean for consumers and businesses?''\par \par \uc2 During the early part of the pandemic recovery, the unemployment rate in California was not an outlier -- 4 percent in May 2022 versus 3.6 percent nationwide, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . But the situation deteriorated.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 36,000 Californians who work in the information industry, which includes tech, lost their jobs last year. Several powerhouse companies based in the state -- \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2  and X, formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2  -- cut tens of thousands of positions to reduce costs as the industry increasingly pivoted its focus toward artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, Snap, the Santa Monica-based parent of the \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  messaging app, announced it would cut about 500 employees, 10 percent of its global work force. And \uc2 Northrop Grumman\uc2 , the aerospace giant, signaled it planned to lay off 1,000 workers in the Los Angeles area.\par \par \uc2 Despite a bruising several months, the unemployment rate in San Francisco and Silicon Valley remained relatively low -- 3.5 percent in the city and 3.2 percent in San Mateo County -- indicating that many workers found new jobs relatively quickly.\par \par \uc2 The outlook is worse in Southern California, where the ripple effects from last year's entertainment industry strikes are still having an impact.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 25,000 workers lost their jobs in Hollywood, according to a report released in December by the Otis College of Art and Design in Los Angeles. While the prolonged work stoppages by the Writers Guild of America and \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  ended last fall, some jobs dependent on the industry never returned, and many people have struggled to land full-time work.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate in Los Angeles County is around 5 percent, with jobs in the information industry, which includes motion picture and sound recording jobs, accounting for a large portion of the hole.\par \par \uc2 During the strikes, some restaurants and other small businesses that relied on Hollywood workers closed for good, and others that scaled back on staff haven't built back to previous levels, said \uc2 Kevin Klowden\uc2 , an executive director at the \uc2 Milken Institute\uc2 , an economic think tank in Santa Monica.\par \par \uc2 A stall in streaming growth has put increased financial pressures on many studios, Mr. Klowden said, adding that ''peak TV production is generally agreed to have already happened even before the strike.''\par \par \uc2 ''There are a lot of stories about actors and crews having trouble finding consistent work because of the slow ramp-up of new productions,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 After a Hollywood strike in 2007-8, it took a year for the industry to recover, and this time -- with persistent losses -- it will take even longer, Mr. Klowden said.\par \par \uc2 For parts of the state where agriculture is a key industry, the economic situation is yet more dire.\par \par \uc2 In Imperial County, a stretch along the Mexican border long known for agricultural production, the latest unemployment rate was about 18 percent, up 3.1 percentage points from a year earlier. And Tulare County, in the Central Valley, has an unemployment rate around 11 percent, up 2.7 percentage points. Automation has been a factor.\par \par \uc2 In a survey released in the fall by the \uc2 Public Policy Institute of California\uc2 , roughly one in four Californians said the availability of well-paying jobs was a big problem in the local area.\par \par \uc2 There are economic bright spots. The state has seen job growth in education and health care, along with the leisure and hospitality industries.\par \par \uc2 ''California is the tent pole of the American economy in terms of American recovery -- in terms of job creation, innovation, entrepreneurial spirit,'' Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  said in January as he unveiled his budget.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Newsom's office released an analysis of the state's economic outlook for the year ahead, noting that ''while unemployment in California may be rising somewhat faster than the nation, it is increasing from an extraordinarily low level, reflective of a tight labor market that is adjusting to more sustainable growth after rebounding so swiftly in the wake of the pandemic-induced recession.''\par \par \uc2 Dee Dee Myers\uc2 , director of the Governor's Office of Business and Economic Development, said in a statement, ''There is ample reason to believe that California's economy will continue to grow more quickly than the nation's.''\par \par \uc2 She noted a recent directive by Mr. Newsom to create a master plan for career education that connects students to job opportunities. One priority is to reduce barriers for people seeking state jobs -- including college degree requirements unnecessary for some duties, according to an outline of the directive.\par \par \uc2 But elevated unemployment will have a ripple effect on the state for a while, said \uc2 Robert Fairlie\uc2 , a professor of economics and public policy at the \uc2 University of California, Los Angeles\uc2 . Joblessness reduces overall earnings, he said, which translates into lower consumer demand and investment.\par \par \uc2 ''There is a negative multiplier effect on the state economy from the higher unemployment rates we are seeing,'' Mr. Fairlie said.\par \par \uc2 Elyse Jackson is among those feeling the pinch.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Jackson, 27, has not had a steady job since December 2022. An art department coordinator on feature films in Los Angeles, she had hoped to find work soon after the strikes ended last fall.\par \par \uc2 ''The rehiring and new productions have just been so slow,'' said Ms. Jackson, a member of the International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees union. She has taken on $15,000 in debt in recent months and struggles to pay the rent on the apartment she shares with her partner in the Echo Park neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 Unable to keep waiting for jobs in her industry, she recently filled out dozens of applications for administrative work around the city. She has yet to hear back.\par \par \uc2 ''In terms of skill sets, I am certainly qualified for these jobs,'' Ms. Jackson said. ''There just seems to be a lot of competition because of the market and unemployment.''\par \par \uc2 Broccoli harvesting in Imperial County, Calif., where the unemployment rate stands at 18 percent, an increase of 3.1 percentage points from a year earlier. (PHOTOGRAPH BY METTE LAMPCOV FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B1, B6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240304ek320000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
