\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Reuters \par \uc2 519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MEDIA\par \par \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  Plunges After Big Loss\par \par \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  reported a bigger-than-expected quarterly loss on Friday, as the media conglomerate battled a weak advertising market and the fallout of the twin Hollywood strikes on content generation.\par \par \uc2 Shares of the company, forged by the union of \uc2 WarnerMedia\uc2  and Discovery, tumbled nearly 12 percent, even as it beat \uc2 Disney\uc2  and Paramount to an inaugural annual profit for the streaming business. Shares of Paramount fell more than 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 The results highlight the challenges after the strikes by writers and actors paralyzed production for months before ending in September and November, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2 's studio business revenue sank 17 percent in the fourth quarter as it had little to follow the success of ''Barbie,'' which released in July and topped $1 billion in ticket sales worldwide.\par \par \uc2 The company is pinning its hopes on the March release of the second installment of sci-fi epic ''Dune,'' featuring Timoth\u233\'00\'E9e Chalamet and Zendaya. The release was delayed from November due to the Hollywood strikes.\par \par \uc2 Advertising revenue at its networks segment declined 12 percent to $1.95 billion, hurt by the ongoing decline in audiences for traditional television and a weaker economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 Overall fourth-quarter revenue of $10.28 billion missed analysts' average estimate of $10.35 billion, per \uc2 LSEG\uc2  data. REUTERS\par \par \uc2 COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 As Paramount Struggles,\par \par \uc2 Its Credit Rating Is in Peril\par \par \uc2 The S&P ratings agency placed \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 's credit rating on a negative watch on Friday, citing low operating cash flow generation at the media conglomerate due to the decline in cable television and competition in streaming.\par \par \uc2 S&P currently has a BBB+ -- investment grade -- rating on the firm, whose stock has lost about a fifth of its value this year in the face of weak revenue growth and a $15.63 billion debt load as of September that was more than its total assets.\par \par \uc2 ''We believe FOCF (free operating cash flow) will be weaker than historical levels because the significant cash flows from the linear TV businesses will degrade rapidly as pay-TV subscribers continue to decline and advertisers migrate spending to streaming platforms,'' the ratings agency said.\par \par \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 's shares were down 5 percent on Friday as rival \uc2 Warner Bros Discovery\uc2  reported bigger-than-expected quarterly loss due a weak advertising market and the impact of Hollywood strikes.\par \par \uc2 The ratings agency said Paramount's cash flow declines have been more than its rivals due to ''its smaller scale, less business diversification, and slower DTC (direct-to-consumer) ramp up.''\par \par \uc2 The cash flow generated from the streaming business will be lower due to higher spending on content, investments in technology, and an increase in marketing costs, S&P said.\par \par \uc2 After pouring millions of dollars into its fast-growing but unprofitable streaming unit Paramount Plus, the company said in November investments in the subsidiary had peaked a year early.\par \par \uc2 Paramount is expected to report fourth-quarter results on Feb. 28. REUTERS\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY ALLISON DINNER/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240224ek2o0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
