\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  Ordered to Reinstate Worker Who Quit Over Wearing B.L.M. Logo\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jesus Jim\u233\'00\'E9nez \par \uc2 831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  said that \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  violated federal law in 2021 when it told a worker that they must quit or stop showing support for the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2  on their apron.\par \par \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  must reinstate a worker who quit after they refused to remove a slogan supporting the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2  from their apron, the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  announced on Wednesday after it found that the worker's actions were protected by federal law.\par \par \uc2 The ruling by the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  held that \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  violated federal law in 2021 when it told the worker that they must quit or remove the letters ''BLM,'' an acronym for \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 , that they had drawn by hand onto their apron.\par \par \uc2 The case is one of several that centered on the issue of civil rights apparel in the workplace after the police killing of George Floyd in May 2020, an episode that galvanized many workers across the country to back the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2  by showing support on their work uniforms or face masks.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  said in its ruling that Antonio Morales Jr., who worked at a \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  store in the Minneapolis area, was protected by the National Labor Relations Act, which guarantees the legal right of workers to take part in ''concerted activities'' for ''mutual aid or protection.''\par \par \uc2 In its ruling, the federal agency said that the worker's refusal to remove the ''BLM'' marking from their uniform was considered to be ''concerted'' and ''for mutual aid or protection'' because of earlier protests by workers at the store about racial discrimination.\par \par \uc2 Lauren McFerran\uc2 , the labor board's chairman, said in a statement on Wednesday that ''it is well-established that workers have the right to join together to improve their working conditions -- including by protesting racial discrimination in the workplace.''\par \par \uc2 She added: ''It is equally clear that an employee who acts individually to support a group protest regarding a workplace issue remains protected under the law.''\par \par \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  said in an emailed statement on Wednesday that it disagreed with the labor board's decision.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 The Home Depot\uc2  is fully committed to diversity and respect for all people,'' the company said. ''We don't tolerate any kind of workplace harassment or discrimination.''\par \par \uc2 The company said in an email that it would review the labor board's full opinion in the coming days. It was unclear whether the company planned to immediately rehire Antonio Morales Jr. or appeal.\par \par \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  can still appeal the labor board's ruling to a federal appeals court.\par \par \uc2 The employee could not immediately be reached for comment on Wednesday. It was unclear whether they had a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 Charlotte Garden, a law professor at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 , said in an interview on Wednesday that the labor's decision in the \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  case had the potential to set a precedent in similar cases before the labor board.\par \par \uc2 ''This decision is extremely important in setting out some principles for how the board is going to deal with these issues,'' Ms. Garden said.\par \par \uc2 Still, other cases will depend on their own individual facts, Ms. Garden said, adding that one distinction in the \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  case was that the worker was wearing the ''BLM'' marking on their uniform as part of an effort to bring attention to racial discrimination at their store.\par \par \uc2 According to the labor board's order, the employee began wearing the ''BLM'' marking in August 2020, and some time after that, they began talking to co-workers and supervisors about ''subjects such as ongoing discrimination and harassment'' at the store.\par \par \uc2 In one instance highlighted in the labor board's order, the worker was told by co-worker to closely follow a Black customer shopping in the store because the co-worker said that people of Somali descent were more likely to steal.\par \par \uc2 On Feb. 18, 2021, Antonio Morales Jr. met with a \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  district manager who told them that wearing the ''BLM'' marking was against company dress code, and that they could not wear the marking on their apron.\par \par \uc2 ''I know that, and I am willing to be fired over this,'' they said, according to a transcript of the meeting.\par \par \uc2 Antonio Morales Jr. resigned the following day, according to the labor board.\par \par \uc2 In addition to reinstating the worker, \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  was also ordered to give them back pay, compensate them for any adverse tax consequences, and the store must also stop prohibiting its workers from taking part in ''protected concerted activities,'' such as wearing a \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  marking on an apron.\par \par \uc2 The National Labor Relations Board ruled that the law was broken when a worker was told to quit or remove a label from an apron worn at the store. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BRANDON BELL/GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240224ek2o00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
