\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 507 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 Wholesale Prices Rise\par \par \uc2 As Inflation Persists\par \par \uc2 Wholesale prices in the U.S. picked up in January, the latest sign that some inflation pressures in the economy remain elevated.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department reported Friday that its producer price index -- which tracks inflation before it reaches consumers -- rose 0.3 percent from December to January after having fallen -0.1 percent from November to December. Measured year over year, producer prices rose by a mild 0.9 percent in January.\par \par \uc2 Excluding volatile food and energy costs, ''core'' wholesale prices rose 0.5 percent, the most since last July. Compared with a year ago, core prices climbed 2 percent, up from 1.7 percent, in the previous month.\par \par \uc2 The costs of hospital care, doctor visits and hotel stays also jumped last month, a sign that inflation in travel and other service industries also remains elevated.\par \par \uc2 Friday's figures will likely underscore the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's caution about when to begin cutting its benchmark interest rate. THE ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  Is Cutting 1,600 JobsTo Help Reduce Costs\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  is cutting 2 percent of its global workforce, or little over 1,600 jobs, as the athletic wear giant aims to cut costs and reinvest its savings into what it sees as big growth areas like sport, health and wellness.\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2 , based in Beaverton, Oregon, joins a growing number of companies, including \uc2 Estee Lauder\uc2  and \uc2 Levi Strauss & Co.\uc2 , that have announced job cuts in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 As of May 31, 2023, \uc2 Nike\uc2  employed roughly 84,000 workers, according to its annual report.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal's website was first to report the cuts.\par \par \uc2 In December, \uc2 Nike\uc2  reduced its annual sales outlook for the fiscal year after reporting second-quarter sales results that fell short of company expectations. The reduced outlook came as the company executives told analysts that it has seen more cautious consumer behavior worldwide in an ''uneven macro environment.'' THE ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 AUTOMOBILES\par \par \uc2 U.A.W. Threatens Strike\par \par \uc2 At Ford Plant in Kentucky\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  is threatening to go on strike next week at \uc2 Ford Motor Co.\uc2 's largest and most profitable factory in a dispute over local contract language.\par \par \uc2 The union said on Friday that nearly 9,000 workers at the Kentucky Truck Plant in Louisville will strike on Feb. 23 if the local contract dispute is not resolved.\par \par \uc2 If there's a strike, it would be the second time the union has walked out at the sprawling factory in the past year. In October, \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers shut down the plant during national contract negotiations that ended with large raises for employees.\par \par \uc2 The union says that workers have been without a local contract for five months. The main areas of dispute are health and safety issues, minimum in-plant nurse staffing, ergonomic issues, and the company's effort to reduce the number of skilled trades workers.\par \par \uc2 THE ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY JEFF CHIU/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240217ek2h0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
