\par\par \uc2 THE FIX\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 How to Make Bedrooms Do Double Duty\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tim McKeough \par \uc2 1,555 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Maybe you're working from home more often -- or you need a place to retreat from the world. Here's how to make your bedroom do double (or triple) duty.\par \par \uc2 Making the most of whatever space you have is just good sense -- whether you've recently bought your dream home or, like many people, are staying put until the housing market calms down.\par \par \uc2 The obvious place to start? Your bedroom.\par \par \uc2 Sure, it's used for sleeping at night, but what about the rest of the time? With a little planning, it could double as a home office -- or maybe a library or a playroom. It could even be a place to make cappuccino.\par \par \uc2 ''It's something we're being asked to do more and more,'' said Sean Anderson, an interior designer based in Memphis. ''A lot of it has stemmed from the shift in the past couple of years to remote work. The bedroom needs to be this place of solitude, but it also needs to be multifunctional. People want a spot to lounge, a spot to work and, obviously, a place to sleep.''\par \par \uc2 William Hefner, an interior designer in Los Angeles, said he gets similar requests from frazzled parents who want their bedrooms to serve as a retreat. ''They can close that door to do some work or some exercise,'' he said. ''Or make some coffee or open a bottle of champagne.''\par \par \uc2 So what's the best way to turn your bedroom into a multifunctional space? He and other designers shared their secrets.\par \par \uc2 Install a Desk\par \par \uc2 The most obvious addition to a bedroom is a place to work.\par \par \uc2 When Nicole Hirsch, a designer in Wellesley, Mass., was tasked with designing a new primary suite for a couple, a desk where they could catch up on email and write letters was at the top of their wish list.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hirsch obliged by opening up an alcove in the bedroom with windows overlooking the yard and adding a custom-made desk with built-in cabinetry -- ''a space for light work.''\par \par \uc2 To maintain a calm feeling in the bedroom, she made sure there were no filing cabinets, printers, whiteboards or computer monitors left out in the open. ''It's more of a casual desk space,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Anderson, the Memphis designer, often uses small-scale desks in the bedrooms he designs to create similar spaces for occasional work. ''So many people are now working with laptops, iPads and phones that having a space for all those devices to land is something they're asking for,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In compact bedrooms, he might place a small desk beside the bed so that it doubles as a night stand. ''When we don't have the real estate for a separate area, it becomes this multifunctional piece,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Use a Murphy Bed\par \par \uc2 If you need more work space -- or the room in question is used for overnight guests -- consider installing a Murphy bed that can be tucked away during the day.\par \par \uc2 Once seen as impractical, Murphy beds have been growing in popularity in recent years, as new mechanisms make them easier to operate, said Mike Cassidy, a director at California Closets.\par \par \uc2 ''Multiuse seems to be a bigger consideration that it was before Covid,'' Mr. Cassidy said. ''As we're settling into whatever this new normal is, the need for multifunctional spaces hasn't slowed down.''\par \par \uc2 The designer Annie Selke, for example, had California Closets install a Murphy bed in a home office at her vacation house in Palm Springs, Calif., which allowed the space to double as a guest room. Ariel Gordon, a jewelry designer, outfitted a backyard work-from-home studio with a Murphy bed from California Closets for the same reason.\par \par \uc2 Create a Lounge\par \par \uc2 How about retreating to your bedroom to cozy up by a fireplace?\par \par \uc2 For one lucky couple, Ms. Hirsch designed a primary bedroom with a pair of chairs and footstools next to a wood-burning fireplace, as well as a seating area with a sofa and coffee table that makes the room feel like a private refuge.\par \par \uc2 In smaller bedrooms, even a generous armchair paired with a small end table and floor lamp can offer a comforting place to read.\par \par \uc2 A bedroom can easily double as a media room as well, but most designers like to hide the TV when it isn't being watched. Ms. Hirsch once concealed a TV behind wooden wall panels above a fireplace. Other designers have used solutions both simple and elaborate -- putting a TV inside an armoire, for instance, or installing a retractable under-bed lift.\par \par \uc2 Make It a Library\par \par \uc2 If you're thinking about putting a reading chair in your bedroom, where will you keep all the books?\par \par \uc2 With the addition of a few bookcases, a bedroom can double as a library. In his own bedroom, in the Hancock Park neighborhood of Los Angeles, Mr. Hefner lined a sunny nook with built-in shelving and cabinets to create a mini library.\par \par \uc2 His best-looking books go on the shelves, to add to the ambience of the room. The ones that aren't so nice to look at, like dog-eared paperbacks, are stored in the cabinets.\par \par \uc2 ''It's just nice to have everything right there,'' he said. ''You don't have to go downstairs to get a book.''\par \par \uc2 Or a Playroom\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hefner often designs multifunctional bedrooms for children, too.\par \par \uc2 For his son, Koji, he furnished a room with a sofa-like bed. The seat cushion is a twin-size mattress, so it offers a place to sleep and to hang out. Across the room, he mounted a shelving unit with a fold-down top, creating a small space for doing homework.\par \par \uc2 In a client's home, where he worked with the interior designer Jeremiah Brent, he went further, placing the bed at the center of the room and then covering the back of the headboard with Lego baseplates and adding a desk behind it for Lego building. On a wall beside the bed, he and Mr. Brent installed climbing grips and a climbing rope leading up to a tiny loft.\par \par \uc2 ''This particular kid loved climbing,'' Mr. Hefner said. ''So we thought, 'OK, we can have this climbing apparatus right in the room, without taking up much floor space.'''\par \par \uc2 Don't Forget Refreshments\par \par \uc2 An emerging trend in bedroom design is including a built-in coffee station or minibar, similar to what you might see in a hotel room.\par \par \uc2 ''It's becoming very common,'' said Katie Fugate, a designer at Maeve Design Collective in Scottsdale, Ariz., noting that the detailing of these refreshment nooks is often as upscale as a high-end kitchen.\par \par \uc2 Recently, when her firm was doing the interiors of a home in Phoenix, the designers added a coffee station to the primary bedroom -- complete with olive-painted cabinets, marble counters, a Wolf coffee maker, a concealed under-counter refrigerator and a bar-size sink. Now the owners can enjoy that first cup of coffee without venturing far from bed.\par \par \uc2 ''The kitchen was pretty far away from the primary bedroom,'' Ms. Fugate said, ''so we wanted to make sure our clients had easy accessibility to a good little coffee bar.''\par \par \uc2 Install a Soaking Tub\par \par \uc2 Another idea borrowed from hotels: installing a bathtub in your bedroom.\par \par \uc2 ''It's really a cool thing to have a stand-alone pedestal tub in a beautiful room,'' said Jason Halter, a principal of the Toronto-based design firm Wonder Incorporated.\par \par \uc2 Since seeing the bathtub-in-bedroom concept at a hotel in Venice, Mr. Halter has designed a few bedrooms with tubs, including one with a tub directly beneath a skylight, a few steps from the bed. The flat roof above is planted with greenery, so bathers see vegetation around the edges of the skylight during the day.\par \par \uc2 But nighttime soaks are even better, Mr. Halter said: ''We thought it would be awesome to be able to have a bath while staring up at the stars.''\par \par \uc2 For weekly email updates on residential real estate news, sign up here.\par \par \uc2 William Hefner, a designer in Los Angeles, put a sofalike bed in his son's room, above left, along with a shelving unit that has a fold-down top. Above right, in a bedroom Nicole Hirsch designed, there's room for a home office in an alcove. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY LAURA HULL; SARAH WINCHESTER STUDIOS); Left, Ariel Gordon worked with California Closets to place a Murphy bed in her backyard studio. Far left, Sean Anderson, a designer in Memphis, put a desk beside the bed, to serve as both night stand and a place to work.; Clockwise from middle: Ms. Hirsch often includes a lounge space in the primary bedrooms she designs; Annie Selke installed a Murphy bed in her Palm Springs, Calif., home office; for a child's room, Mr. Hefner and the interior designer Jeremiah Brent added a Lego building station and climbing grips up to a tiny loft; Maeve Design Collective installed a coffee bar as part of a bedroom renovation; Wonder Incorporated added a tub near the bed and a skylight. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ERICKA McCONNELL; HARIS KENJAR; SARAH WINCHESTER STUDIOS; SEAN McLAUGHLIN; MATTHEW WILLIAMS; MICHAEL CLIFFORD; TREVOR TONDRO) This article appeared in print on page RE8. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240204ek240006k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
