\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Dossier Details Alleged Actions Of Aid Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ronen Bergman and Patrick Kingsley \par \uc2 1,327 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Israeli officials have presented evidence they say ties workers at a Palestinian aid agency in Gaza to violence during the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 -led attack on Israel.\par \par \uc2 One is accused of kidnapping a woman. Another is said to have handed out ammunition. A third was described as taking part in the massacre at a kibbutz where 97 people died. And all were said to be employees of the \uc2 United Nations\uc2  aid agency that schools, shelters and feeds hundreds of thousands of Palestinians in the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 The accusations are contained in a dossier provided to the \uc2 United States government\uc2  that details Israel's claims against a dozen employees of the \uc2 United Nations Relief and Works Agency\uc2  who, it says, played a role in the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks against Israel on Oct. 7 or in their aftermath.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  said on Friday that it had fired several employees after being briefed on the allegations. But little was known about the accusations until the dossier was reviewed on Sunday by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The accusations are what prompted eight countries, including the United States, to suspend some aid payment to the \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 , as the agency is known, even as war plunges Palestinians in Gaza into desperate straits. More than 26,000 people have been killed there and nearly two million displaced, according to Gazan and \uc2 U.N.\uc2  officials.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  workers have been accused of helping \uc2 Hamas\uc2  stage the attack that set off the war in Gaza, or of aiding it in the days after. Some 1,200 people in Israel were killed that day, Israeli officials say, and about 240 were abducted and taken to Gaza.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, the \uc2 United Nations\uc2  secretary general, \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2 , described himself as ''horrified by these accusations'' and noted that nine of the 12 accused employees had been fired. But Mr. Guterres implored those nations that had suspended their aid payments to reconsider. \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  is one of the largest employers in Gaza, with 13,000 people, mostly Palestinians, on staff.\par \par \uc2 Asked about Israel's allegations on Sunday, \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  said that two of the 12 employees were dead but that it could not provide more information while the U.N.'s Office of Internal Oversight Services was still investigating.\par \par \uc2 Two Western officials confirmed on the condition of anonymity that they had been briefed on the contents of the dossier in recent days, but said they had not been able to verify the details. Although the United States has yet to corroborate the Israeli claims itself, American officials say they found them credible enough to warrant suspending aid.\par \par \uc2 The Times verified the identity of one of the 12 employees, a storeroom manager, whose social media profile lists him as an \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  employee and shows him wearing U.N.-branded clothes.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli dossier, presented to U.S. officials on Friday, lists the names and jobs of the \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  employees and the allegations against them.\par \par \uc2 The dossier said that Israeli intelligence officers had established the movements of six of the men inside Israel on Oct. 7 based on their phones; others had been monitored while making phone calls inside Gaza during which, the Israelis say, they discussed their involvement in the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack.\par \par \uc2 Three others got text messages ordering them to report to muster points on Oct. 7, and one was told to bring rocket-propelled grenades stored at his home, according to the dossier.\par \par \uc2 The Israelis described 10 of the employees as members of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , the militant group that controlled Gaza at the time of the Oct. 7 attack. Another was said to be affiliated with another militant group, Islamic Jihad.\par \par \uc2 Yet seven of the accused were also said to be teachers at \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  schools, instructing students in subjects like math and Arabic. Two others worked at the schools in other capacities. The remaining three were described as a clerk, a social worker and the storeroom manager.\par \par \uc2 The most detailed accusations in the dossier concerned a school counselor from Khan Younis, in southern Gaza, who is accused of working with his son to abduct a woman from Israel.\par \par \uc2 A social worker from Nuseirat, in central Gaza, is accused of helping to bring the body of a dead Israeli soldier to Gaza, as well as distributing ammunition and coordinating vehicles on the day of the attack.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli accusations come against the backdrop of decades-long frictions with \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 . Since 1949, the agency has cared for the families of Palestinians who fled or were forced from their homes during the wars surrounding the creation of the state of Israel.\par \par \uc2 The organization provides vital aid to more than five million Palestinian refugees scattered across the Middle East, whose future and status have never been resolved despite years of negotiations.\par \par \uc2 But to its critics, including many Israelis, the agency is an obstacle to resolving the conflict. Its very existence, they say, prevents Palestinian refugees from integrating into new communities and stokes their dreams of one day returning to what is now Israel -- a goal that Israel says it will never allow. And in Gaza, Israel argues, \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  has fallen under the influence of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , a claim the agency rejects.\par \par \uc2 This is not the first time the United States has cut off money to the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  agency. The Trump administration suspended aid as part its efforts to pressure the Palestinian leadership to stop demanding that refugees be allowed to return to Israel.\par \par \uc2 But the current threat to its funding is considered to be the gravest in its history because it comes at a time of crisis for Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Amid warnings of famine, the collapse of the health system and the massive displacement of the Palestinian population, \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 's work is considered more important than ever. It helps coordinate the distribution of the supplies of aid -- however meager -- that arrive each day in southern Gaza, and its schools provide shelter to more than a million Gazans, according to the agency's statistics.\par \par \uc2 The funding suspensions may be felt quickly. Unlike other \uc2 U.N.\uc2  agencies, \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  has no strategic financial reserve. On Sunday, Mr. Guterres said services might need to be reduced beginning in February.\par \par \uc2 A day earlier, the agency's commissioner-general, \uc2 Philippe Lazzarini\uc2 , warned of impending catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 ''It would be immensely irresponsible to sanction an agency and an entire community it serves because of allegations of criminal acts against some individuals, especially at a time of war, displacement and political crises in the region,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 ''The lives of people in Gaza depend on this support, and so does regional stability,'' Mr. Lazzarini said.\par \par \uc2 The State Department on Friday acknowledged the critical humanitarian role played by \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  but said it was suspending its funding while it assessed both the allegations and the agency's response to them.\par \par \uc2 Israeli officials themselves were concerned on Sunday about whether their accusations might in the end make their own position more difficult, according to three officials involved in the discussion. A collapse in the delivery of services to Gaza could force Israel to shoulder a greater role in running aid distribution -- a role it does not want.\par \par \uc2 Reports about the accusations against the aid workers came on the same day that the \uc2 International Court of Justice\uc2  issued an interim ruling on accusations of genocide that have been leveled against Israel by South Africa. The court ordered Israel to take action to prevent acts of genocide by its forces in Gaza and to allow more aid into the territory.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Johnatan Reiss, Julian E. Barnes, Gabby Sobelman and Myra Noveck.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Johnatan Reiss, Julian E. Barnes, Gabby Sobelman and Myra Noveck.\par \par \uc2 A UNRWA school in Rafah, south Gaza. The U.N. has asked nations to reconsider aid pauses for the agency following Israel's claims. (PHOTOGRAPH BY HAITHAM IMAD/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) (A6) This article appeared in print on page A1, A6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240129ek1t0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
