\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court to Weigh Starbucks Labor Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 769 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The coffee chain has challenged a federal judge's order to reinstate a group of union activists who were fired at a store in Memphis.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court agreed on Friday to hear a case brought by \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  challenging a federal judge's order to reinstate seven employees who were fired at a store in Memphis amid a union campaign there.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  argued that the criteria for such intervention by judges in labor cases, which can also include measures like reopening shuttered stores, vary across regions of the country because federal appeals courts may adhere to different standards.\par \par \uc2 A regional director for the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , the company's opponent in the case, argued that the apparent differences in criteria among appeals courts were semantic rather than substantive, and that a single effective standard was already in place nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The labor board had urged the Supreme Court to stay out of the case, whose outcome could affect union organizing across the country.\par \par \uc2 The agency asks federal judges for temporary relief, like reinstatement of fired workers, because litigating charges of unfair labor practices can take years. The agency argues that retaliation against workers can have a chilling effect on organizing in the meantime, even if the workers ultimately win their case.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Friday, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said, ''We are pleased the Supreme Court has decided to consider our request to level the playing field for all U.S. employers by ensuring that a single standard is applied as federal district courts.''\par \par \uc2 The labor board declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The union organizing campaign at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  began in the Buffalo area in 2021 and quickly spread to other states. The union, Workers United, represents workers at more than 370 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores, out of roughly 9,600 company-owned stores in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The labor board has issued dozens of complaints against the company based on hundreds of accusations of labor law violations, including threats and retaliation against workers who are seeking to unionize and a failure to bargain in good faith. This week, the agency issued a complaint accusing the company of unilaterally changing work hours and schedules in unionized stores around the country.\par \par \uc2 The company has denied violating labor law and said in a statement that it contested the latest complaint and planned ''to defend our lawful business decisions'' before a judge.\par \par \uc2 The case that led to the dispute before the Supreme Court involves seven workers who were fired in February 2022 after they let local journalists into a closed store to conduct interviews. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said the incident violated company rules; the workers and the union said the company did not enforce such rules against workers who were not involved in union organizing.\par \par \uc2 The labor board found merit in the workers' accusations and issued a complaint two months later. A federal judge granted the labor board's request for an order reinstating the workers that August, and a federal appeals court upheld the order.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is seeking a bailout for its illegal union-busting from Trump's Supreme Court,'' Workers United said in a statement on Friday. ''There's no doubt that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  broke federal law by firing workers in Memphis for joining together in a union.''\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it was critical for the Supreme Court to wade into the case because the labor board was becoming more ambitious in asking judges to order remedies like reinstatement of fired workers.\par \par \uc2 The labor board noted in its filing with the Supreme Court that it was bringing fewer injunctions overall than in some recent years -- only 21 were authorized in 2022, down from more than 35 in 2014 and 2015.\par \par \uc2 A Supreme Court decision could in principle raise the bar for judges to issue orders reinstating workers, effectively limiting the labor board's ability to win temporary relief for workers during a union campaign.\par \par \uc2 The case is not the only recent challenge to the labor board's authority. After the board issued a complaint accusing the rocket company \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  of illegally firing eight employees for criticizing its chief executive, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the company filed a lawsuit this month arguing that the agency's setup for adjudicating complaints is unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 The company said in its lawsuit that the agency's structure violated its right to a trial by jury.\par \par \uc2 A group of former Starbucks employees celebrated the result of a vote to unionize one of the coffee chain's stores in Memphis in 2022. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ADRIAN SAINZ/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240115ek1f0003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
