\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Food Delivery Workers, Overlooked in Life, Are Honored in Death\b0\par\par\uc2 By Orlando Mayorquin \par \uc2 1,625 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page chronicles the deaths of dozens of bike-riding workers who delivered food in New York City, lionizing them as fallen heroes.\par \par \uc2 After the brass band packed up its instruments, Sergio Solano and two other food delivery workers walked a white bicycle to an overpass within view of the United Nations headquarters.\par \par \uc2 A fellow worker, or compa\u241\'00\'F1ero, as they call each other meaning ''partner,'' had died less than two weeks earlier that September in yet another bicycle wreck on the streets of Manhattan. Delivering food has proved to be a deadly occupation for many of them. Riding bikes at all hours, they get hit by cars and are at constant risk of having accidents and falling prey to crime.\par \par \uc2 The spray-painted bicycle paid homage to F\u233\'00\'E9lix Patricio Te\u243\'00\'F3filo, a Mexican immigrant who, like them, made his living pedaling to deliver food. They chained it to the metal railing near the intersection of 47th Street and First Avenue, where he met his end.\par \par \uc2 With that solemn march through the drizzle, Mr. Solano, 39, was adjourning an evening of mourning, fulfilling what he has come to see as a mission: illuminating in death lives that were relegated to the shadows.\par \par \uc2 ''We never thought we would be organizing vigils,'' Mr. Solano said. ''That was never our objective.''\par \par \uc2 Just over three years ago, Mr. Solano and relatives who are also delivery workers started ''El Diario de Los Deliveryboys en La Gran Manzana,'' which translates to ''The Journal of the Deliveryboys in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 ,'' a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page with aims both practical and informative.\par \par \uc2 The page would act as an online support network, a space to alert of bicycle thefts, traffic accidents and discriminatory encounters reported by Spanish-speaking immigrants who brave the urban frenzy to satisfy a New Yorker's takeout cravings.\par \par \uc2 Along the way, it would chronicle the job's twists and turns.\par \par \uc2 Soon after the page was up and running it became clear to Mr. Solano that the project would tell a bigger story: Compa\u241\'00\'F1eros die regularly on the job.\par \par \uc2 More than 40 have died since the page went live in late 2020, by Mr. Solano's latest count.\par \par \uc2 In Mr. Patricio's case, he hit his head on a curb without a helmet in a solo crash.\par \par \uc2 Food delivery workers were for a brief period celebrated in New York as the Covid-19 pandemic drove life indoors and their services became critical.\par \par \uc2 Delivery apps offered viable income to those who had been laid off from their jobs or had their hours slashed, and for those whose immigration status complicated obtaining government aid.\par \par \uc2 As the pandemic lurched on, the dangers of the in-demand work became glaring. Activists formed unions and pushed for better pay and protections, an effort that continued into 2023. Under pressure, the city set a higher minimum wage for app-based delivery workers, starting at about $18 per hour in October.\par \par \uc2 Still, the risk for many workers has gone beyond wages. On the Deliveryboys page, a stream of photos bears the names and faces of the fallen.\par \par \uc2 Most of them are immigrants from Mexico or Guatemala who are part of an estimated cavalry of 65,000 food delivery workers in New York City.\par \par \uc2 The job has become one of the deadliest.\par \par \uc2 A report by the city published in November 2022 said that the fatality rate among food delivery workers who don't use a car was 36 deaths per 100,000 thousand workers from January 2021 to June 2022. That rate surpassed that of workers in construction (seven deaths per 100,000), which had historically been the deadliest industry.\par \par \uc2 Funerals, vigils, death anniversaries and requiems have been organized, fund-raised and digitally inscribed into the community's memory by the self-titled journal.\par \par \uc2 Many have died in traffic accidents while on the job. Some of the deaths are not related to work. Others, like Francisco Villalva, have been murdered.\par \par \uc2 In March 2021, an assailant who was after Mr. Villalva's bicycle shot him at a park near 108th Street and Third Avenue in Manhattan. Mr. Villalva, of Xalpatlahuac, Guerrero, in southwestern Mexico, was 29.\par \par \uc2 Two days later, the page streamed live video from the spot of the killing, calling for others to support the family. Relatives who appeared in the video spoke in both Spanish and Nahuatl, an Indigenous language spoken in parts of Mexico. (To date, the video has more than 132,000 views.) They also called for justice.\par \par \uc2 ''Unfortunately, another compa\u241\'00\'F1ero has lost his life doing this job,'' C\u233\'00\'E9sar Solano, Mr. Solano's nephew, and also a page administrator, said in Spanish, relating the news with a television reporter's cadence.\par \par \uc2 The Deliveryboys page's follower count has surged from hundreds to thousands, affording the platform some mobilizing power.\par \par \uc2 ''For almost a month, we did protests,'' Sergio Solano said. ''We did vigils upon vigils upon vigils. People would come offering to donate food or provide live music. Every day that we did something, a ton of people would come.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Villalva's death had galvanized the community. Compa\u241\'00\'F1eros paused their delivery apps to attend the events. A Catholic priest was brought in to lead prayers. Family and friends arranged for food. Others picked up instruments.\par \par \uc2 One group wrote Mr. Villalva his own corrido, a Mexican folk ballad, telling of his journey in New York through its unnerving end.\par \par \uc2 The killer, identified as Douglas Young, was caught and eventually convicted of murder. In April, Mr. Young, a 41-year-old man from Queens, was sentenced to serve 41 years to life in state prison.\par \par \uc2 Since Mr. Villalva's death, the page has helped ensure that each fallen compa\u241\'00\'F1ero is given a remembrance -- a practice that has become almost ritualistic, reminiscent of the farewells to police officers killed in the line of duty.\par \par \uc2 The loved ones bear the brunt of the organizing, Sergio Solano said, but the page, which is 51,000 followers strong, brings people out.\par \par \uc2 At Mr. Patricio's vigil, C\u233\'00\'E9sar Solano, 22, livestreamed the band's truncated sidewalk performance. Police officers fielding a noise complaint gave them 10 minutes to play their tribute.\par \par \uc2 Under a makeshift canopy, dozens unhusked pork tamales, sipped on atole de pi\u241\'00\'F1a (a pineapple-flavored corn beverage) and slurped steamy pozole from flimsy foam bowls, abiding each aching note: a folksy interpretation of Chopin's ''Marche Fun\u232\'00\'E8bre'' and traditional Mexican funeral songs such as ''Te vas \u225\'00\'E1ngel m\u237\'00\'EDo'' (''You're Leaving My Angel'').\par \par \uc2 Mr. Patricio's sister, Jovita Patricio, buried her face on a friend's chest. A tear parted her reddened cheek. Behind her, the candlelight caressed her brother's portrait, encircled by flowers. He was her only relative in New York.\par \par \uc2 The video stream of the band's performance drew thousands of views. One of the musicians, Edgar Cano, had at one point worked with Mr. Patricio at a restaurant, and they both hailed from the same area in Guerrero.\par \par \uc2 ''We never know. Today or tomorrow, another friend can pass,'' Mr. Cano said in Spanish, his sombrero casting a shadow over his eyes.\par \par \uc2 Some find the page's exhaustive posts invasive.\par \par \uc2 But Sergio Solano said the page's focus and the tributes honor the fallen delivery workers with ''a proper final goodbye'' and give loved ones a chance to grieve from afar. ''If they loved and adored him back home, we show that he was loved and adored here as well,'' he said in Spanish.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, the page carries live video of the arrival of a compa\u241\'00\'F1ero's body in their pueblo. Mr. Villalva's return, for instance, was shown in a livestream.\par \par \uc2 Last summer, when Eduardo Valencia, 28, was killed in an accident while he was working, his story, too, became the focus of the Deliveryboys page.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Valencia had come to the city from Guerrero as a teen, said his mother, Guadalupe Nepomuceno. His dream was to save up enough to carve out a comfortable living in his hometown, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''He wanted to build his house, return to Mexico and never return to New York,'' Ms. Nepomuceno said in Spanish.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Valencia's homecoming would be inside a coffin.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Nepomuceno, who lives in New York City, could not attend her son's burial, casting her final goodbye from a small digital screen more than 2,000 miles away.\par \par \uc2 The efforts serve as recognition for people who are often overlooked, Sergio Solano said.\par \par \uc2 ''In the eyes of society, they don't exist,'' he said. ''They start to exist when you start to give them visibility.''\par \par \uc2 As city life regains its prepandemic rhythms, Mr. Solano added, food delivery workers have faded into the background.\par \par \uc2 Planting a ''ghost bicycle,'' as memorials for cyclists are known, at the spot of a compa\u241\'00\'F1ero's death is a way to tell of deliveryboys' contributions and the ultimate price some pay.\par \par \uc2 With Mr. Patricio's memorial secured, Mr. Solano and two compa\u241\'00\'F1eros donned helmets, mounted bicycles and crept toward the intersection. They looked both ways for passing cars.\par \par \uc2 It was forty past seven on a Monday evening. Time to get to work.\par \par \uc2 A bicycle painted white is used as a memorial for F\u233\'00\'E9lix Patricio Te\u243\'00\'F3filo in Manhattan, near where he was killed. At left, Sergio Solano, left, who started an online page to report on matters affecting delivery drivers.; A vigil for Mr. Te\u243\'00\'F3filo, a food delivery worker who died after hitting his head on a curb in a solo crash. The delivery industry, between accidents and violence, has become one of New York's City's deadliest. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JEENAH MOON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240113ek1d0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
