\par\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 Unions' Backing for Israel Shifts to Protests\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg and Santul Nerkar \par \uc2 2,020 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The shift reflects a broader generational change, and has exposed philosophical rifts in the labor movement about what the purpose of a union ought to be.\par \par \uc2 ''Why are we here?'' said Brandon Mancilla, a leader with the \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2 . Mr. Mancilla faced a crowd of hundreds of union members gathered on the steps of the \uc2 New York Public Library\uc2 's Fifth Avenue branch, huddling against the cold as they rallied for a cease-fire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 ''Cease-fire now, solidarity forever!'' Mr. Mancilla, 29, said as the crowd cheered, waving union banners and Palestinian flags. ''Let's get more and more unions behind us.''\par \par \uc2 On display in that Dec. 21 protest -- which came shortly after the 350,000 member U.A.W. voted to support a cease-fire -- was a shift in the American labor movement's relationship with Israel.\par \par \uc2 For decades, the most prominent American unions were largely supportive of Israel. Today, though, amid a resurgence of the American labor movement, some activists are urging their unions to call for an immediate cease-fire in Gaza and succeeding -- a change that reflects a broader generational shift.\par \par \uc2 But many unions are divided over what stance to take or whether to take any stance at all.\par \par \uc2 Some American labor leaders have remained supportive of Israel's war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and moved swiftly to condemn \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's attacks on Oct. 7. They are dismayed by the views of a younger generation of organizers who in some cases oppose Israel's existence as a Jewish state.\par \par \uc2 ''There has been a shift in society, and that's reflected in the labor movement as it is every place else,'' said \uc2 Stuart Appelbaum\uc2 , president of the Jewish Labor Committee and head of the Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union.\par \par \uc2 Union Support for Israel\par \par \uc2 The American labor movement's traditionally close relationship with Israel stems from decades of Jewish labor leaders staunchly backing the state, even before its founding. In 1917, the American Federation of Labor passed a resolution supporting the Balfour Declaration, which called for the creation of a Jewish state in Palestine, and throughout the 1920s and '30s unions donated millions to Histadrut, Israel's national labor union.\par \par \uc2 After Israel's founding in 1948, American unions started investing in the country's bond program, using money from strike and pension funds. Some also donated money to build stadiums and children's homes in Israel. By 1994, $1 billion had been invested in those bonds by around 1,700 American trade unions, according to archival research from Jeff Schuhrke, a labor historian at Empire State University.\par \par \uc2 ''In many ways, you can argue that U.S. unions helped construct the state of Israel,'' Mr. Schuhrke said.\par \par \uc2 In 1980, the president of the A.F.L.-C.I.O., Lane Kirkland, declared that a Palestinian state would be ''a terrorist state'' and ''an unmixed disaster to the United States'' before hundreds of labor lawyers and union officials. In 1982, the union took out an advertisement in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  declaring support for Israel in its war against Lebanon: ''The A.F.L.-C.I.O. is not neutral.''\par \par \uc2 Union support for Israel sometimes bred internal tensions, with some of the union rank-and-file membership protesting the relationship. In 1949, the same year the International Ladies Garment Worker Union made a $1 million Israeli bond purchase, a group of its members asked the union to support Palestinian refugees. In 1973, thousands of Arab American auto workers in Detroit briefly walked off the job to protest the U.A.W.'s financial support of Israel. In 2002, after John J. Sweeney, then the A.F.L.-C.I.O. president, spoke at the National Rally for Israel, a group of union members circulated a petition condemning his support for the country.\par \par \uc2 Professional Consequences\par \par \uc2 Since the Israel-Gaza war broke out, debates over the fighting have exposed deeper rifts over how unions should represent their diverse membership, and how to balance political advocacy with professional ramifications.\par \par \uc2 The Writers Guild faced an outpouring of frustration from more than 300 members when the union didn't immediately condemn \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's attacks on Oct. 7. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and its union, Starbucks Workers United, are suing each other over the union's use of company imagery in a pro-Palestinian social media post. Chris Smalls, head of the Amazon Labor Union, drew backlash for a pro-Palestinian post that included the phrase ''from the river to the sea,'' -- a decades-old Palestinian nationalist slogan that many see as a call for Israel's annihilation -- echoing an outcry The New Yorker's union faced in 2021 when it posted the phrase on social media.\par \par \uc2 When a proposed statement calling for a cease-fire circulated in early November at the Association of Legal Aid Attorneys, a U.A.W. sub-union of more than 3,000 public defenders and legal workers, a heated internal debate broke out. Those who opposed it said they didn't understand why the union had to weigh in on the issue, which had little direct connection to their work.\par \par \uc2 One legal aid lawyer, Isaac Altman, said in an interview that he found the union's proposed resolution to be one-sided. He said he couldn't understand why the resolution did not shine a greater spotlight on the violence of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants. But he was also concerned that the statement would anger the legal establishment in Nassau County, potentially posing a harm to the clients he represents.\par \par \uc2 So he, along with three other legal aid lawyers, sued to stop the union from voting on a cease-fire resolution. A court issued a temporary injunction.\par \par \uc2 ''I felt there was a real concern that judges would look negatively at this resolution and take it out on our clients,'' said Mr. Altman, 27, who is Jewish.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Altman's organization, the Legal Aid Society of Nassau County, gets its funding from contracts with the Republican-controlled county. He and his colleagues worried about how the proposed resolution might affect their funding.\par \par \uc2 ''We have a higher duty or obligation to our clients that I think trumps people's right to speak,'' said Ilana Kopmar, another plaintiff in the suit and a legal aid lawyer for 31 years. Ms. Kopmar said she was also worried about the impact such a statement could have on Jewish and Israeli clients.\par \par \uc2 The injunction was dissolved on Dec. 15 by a federal judge in the Eastern District of New York, and a few days later, the cease-fire statement passed, by a vote of 1,067 to 570.\par \par \uc2 The president of the Neighborhood Defender Service union, which is also part of the legal aid lawyers union, resigned on Nov. 6 over concerns that his organization's funding would be at risk if it released a statement on the war. \uc2 The Bronx Defenders\uc2 , another public defense organization in New York, faced calls to be defunded across the city after its union released a statement in support of Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 Other union organizers worry about the internal tensions provoked by these debates. NewsGuild-CWA, which is the parent organization of \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 's union, and represents more than 26,000 members as part of the \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2 , heard from some of its members who wanted the union to take a position in favor of a cease-fire. A group of journalists at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , concerned this would undermine coverage of the war, then formed an Independence Caucus.\par \par \uc2 ''We want the Guild leadership to avoid public positions that compromise the journalistic independence required of many members and could undermine our work,'' said Megan Twohey, a leader in the caucus.\par \par \uc2 The union, which represents workers outside the media industry too, has not taken a position on the war. It has made statements about journalists killed in the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Within some newer unions, there's been a reticence to weigh in on a cease-fire. The Alphabet Workers Union, for example, at \uc2 Google\uc2 's parent company \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , which has about 1,400 members, hasn't voted on whether to call for a cease-fire, partly because the union is nascent and worried about alienating potential members, particularly tech workers in Israel. The issue has come up for discussion at membership meetings and on the messaging app \uc2 Discord\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A Generational Shift\par \par \uc2 Changes in union attitudes toward Israel are coming at a moment of wider revival for the American labor movement. After strikes in Hollywood and at auto plants, public approval of unions stood at 67 percent last year, up from 54 percent a decade ago, according to Gallup.\par \par \uc2 ''For a few decades, coinciding with the labor movement's decline, its vision became much more narrow,'' said Mr. Mancilla, the U.A.W. leader. ''It was on the defensive.''\par \par \uc2 Now, many activists are eager to see their unions seize on the momentum of this period by taking bold stances on progressive issues, which they see as part of a history of American labor's involvement in national and international politics. (Labor unions helped organize the 1963 March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom, which tied demands for fair wages and civil rights.)\par \par \uc2 ''People who have become very engaged in their unions want their unions to be the full expression of their politics,'' said Rebecca Givan, an associate professor of labor studies at Rutgers. ''There's always a risk that can alienate some people, and it is likely to energize others.''\par \par \uc2 And some union organizers feel that if they're taking on political fights at home, they should be taking on battles abroad as well.\par \par \uc2 Peter Lyngso, 30, a part-time package handler at \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  and a Chicago union activist, drew a parallel between younger union members' push for a new contract and their urge to speak out on the conflict. ''What you're seeing is this new activist layer saying these are one and the same fight.''\par \par \uc2 Longtime labor experts say that the demand for pro-cease-fire statements from American unions is evidence of a generational shift. There's a new wave of leadership from young activists who grew up after the Oslo Peace process of the 1990s collapsed.\par \par \uc2 ''This is being generated by social-movement young people, Gen Zs, millennials,'' said Seth Goldstein, a labor lawyer who has worked with the Amazon Labor Union. '' I don't think they're anti-Israel, necessarily. But what they've seen in Israel is the Netanyahu government.''\par \par \uc2 At the same time, some American labor leaders remain adamant about their support for Israel, including Mr. Appelbaum of the Jewish Labor Committee.\par \par \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , 66, president of the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2 , is a longtime supporter of Israel. She went to Israel after the war broke out to meet with civil society groups, pay shiva calls and visit the families of hostages.\par \par \uc2 On Dec. 18, dozens of protesters outside the \uc2 Museum of the City of New York\uc2  demanded that Ms. Weingarten call for a cease-fire. She has since posted that she supports ''a bilateral, negotiated ceasefire'' that brings the hostages home, provides aid to Gaza and ''starts the process of 2 states for 2 peoples.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Weingarten said she felt it was important for unions to engage with geopolitical issues, beyond their contract negotiations, even when labor leaders are faced with protest and dissent.\par \par \uc2 ''There will be people within the labor movement that say, 'Just do the economics, just do collective bargaining,'' she said. ''Then there are people within the labor movement that say intersectionality is imperative.''\par \par \uc2 In December, United Auto Workers members in New York joined other protesters marching for a cease-fire in Gaza. The shift in union sentiment about Israel reflects a broader generational change. (BU4); United Automobile Workers at a December rally outside the New York Public Library on Fifth Avenue, calling for a cease-fire in Gaza. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ANDRES KUDACKI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (BU5) This article appeared in print on page BU1, BU4, BU5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240107ek170005d\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpa